desc: Spectral Vowel Morpher - voice<->wash capture instrument
// =====================================================================
// Capture-based vocal instrument. Feeds on LIVE audio playing into it (drag a
// WAV onto the track, or send another track in -- the proper REAPER way).
// Capture a moment into a slot; it's analyzed two ways at once:
//   * harmonic (voice) -- pitch-detected additive resynth: keeps the vowel
//   * spectral (wash)   -- PaulStretch-style phase randomization: breathy bed
// TEXTURE crossfades between them (0 = voice, 100 = wash). Capture up to 8
// slots and MORPH between them. Captures persist across save/reopen.
//
// USE: put audio on the track. Input level up / Voice down -> you hear source.
// When you hear the moment, hit Capture. (Then sweep CAPTURE POINT by ear to
// land exactly on the vowel -- it re-analyzes live, compensating for the
// fraction of a second between hearing and pressing.) Pull Input down, Voice
// up, set Texture, and Morph between slots.
//
//   Capture point = where in the grab to analyze (0 = earliest .. 100 = at press).
//                   PER SLOT: each slot remembers its own point, so scrubbing one
//                   slot never retunes the others. Switching Capture slot shows
//                   that slot's saved point.
//   Texture       = 0 voice (harmonic) .. 100 wash (phase-randomized)
//   Wash grain   = short = rough/grainy, long = glassy/smooth (wash character)
//   Spread        = spectral blur (Hz);  Audition = monitor focused slot / morph
//   Auto-morph    = Off / Sweep (back+forth) / Glide once (first->last slot) / Shuffle (random-order wander)
//   Low / High cut = band-limit the resynth (High cut also thins the voice's
//                   partials, and sits at an absolute frequency so a Layer
//                   transposes underneath it rather than dragging it along).
//   Layers        = pitch-shifted copies of the SAME morph position -- an
//                   octave each way up to four, plus 3 free-interval Custom
//                   layers -- so an octave stack doesn't clash the way two
//                   instances on Shuffle do. Each level is a plain dB that
//                   means what it says; nothing rebalances behind you.
//   Drift         = suite-wide automation replacement: pick a parameter, and it
//                   wanders on its own (up/down amounts + a wall-clock period),
//                   so a mouse-free / screen-reader user gets evolving motion
//                   without drawing REAPER automation. All targets drift at once.
// =====================================================================

slider1:0<0,7,1>Capture slot
slider2:0<0,1,1{Off,Capture now}>Capture spectrum
slider3:0<0,100,1>Capture point (per slot: earliest .. at press)
slider4:0<-60,12,0.1>Input level (dry, dB)
// Named "Voice level" since this plugin was born, and wrong the whole time: it
// is applied to voutL/voutR, which is the equal-power crossfade of the voice
// AND the wash, after every layer has been summed into both. So it has always
// scaled the wash too, and now it scales all fifteen layers as well. The only
// thing it does not touch is the dry input, which has its own Input level.
// Relabelled rather than re-scoped -- the behaviour is right, the name was not.
slider5:0<-60,12,0.1>Output level (dB, everything but the dry input)
slider6:50<0,100,1>Texture (0 voice, 100 wash)
slider7:150<5,680,1>Wash grain (ms)
slider8:0<0,150,0.5>Spread (Hz)
slider9:0<-96,96,0.1>Pitch (semitones)
slider10:50<0,100,1>Stereo width
slider11:0<0,500,1>Low cut (Hz)
slider12:0<0,100,0.1>Denoise
slider13:1<0,1,1{Focused slot,Morph}>Audition
slider14:0<0,100,0.1>Morph (across captured slots)
slider15:0<0,3,1{Off,Sweep (back+forth),Glide once,Shuffle (random)}>Auto-morph
slider16:20<1,600,0.5>Auto-morph time (sec)
// --- Drift: suite-wide automation replacement (nested-selector pattern).
//     Pick a target; it wanders by up/down amounts over a wall-clock period.
//     All targets drift in parallel; the selector only chooses which one the
//     amount/period/shape sliders are editing. Added at the END of the range
//     (slider IDs are primary keys — never renumber existing ones). ---
slider17:0<0,23,1{Texture,Spread,Pitch,Stereo width,Low cut,Output level,Overtone harmonic,High cut,Original level,Custom 1 level,Custom 2 level,Custom 3 level,4 octaves down level,3 octaves down level,2 octaves down level,1 octave down level,a fifth down level,a fourth down level,a fourth up level,a fifth up level,1 octave up level,2 octaves up level,3 octaves up level,4 octaves up level}>Drift target
slider18:0<0,300,0.1>Drift up amount (units match target)
slider19:0<0,300,0.1>Drift down amount (units match target)
slider20:30<1,600,0.5>Drift period (seconds)
slider21:0<0,2,1{Sine,Triangle,Random}>Drift shape
// Restart on play = drift jumps to the start of its cycle when the transport
// begins (so multiple tracks reset together and stay in sync). Free-running =
// drift ignores the transport and evolves continuously (loop under/over an
// unbroken drift). A loop repeat is never a restart in either mode.
slider22:0<0,1,1{Restart on play,Free-running}>Drift restart
// --- Ramp: the suite's "Speed Ramp" under an honest name (this plugin has no
//     speed to ramp). A one-time slow RIDE of a parameter: pick a target, how
//     far to move it (signed) and over how long, arm Engage, and it glides there
//     once and holds. Nested-selector like Drift; all targets ride in parallel
//     off one Engage; composes with Drift (base + drift wander + ramp ride). ---
slider23:0<0,23,1{Texture,Spread,Pitch,Stereo width,Low cut,Output level,Overtone harmonic,High cut,Original level,Custom 1 level,Custom 2 level,Custom 3 level,4 octaves down level,3 octaves down level,2 octaves down level,1 octave down level,a fifth down level,a fourth down level,a fourth up level,a fifth up level,1 octave up level,2 octaves up level,3 octaves up level,4 octaves up level}>Ramp target
slider24:0<-300,300,0.1>Ramp by (units match target)
slider25:0<0,60,0.1>Ramp duration (minutes)
slider26:0<0,60,0.1>Ramp start delay (minutes)
slider27:0<0,1,1{Off,On}>Ramp engage
// --- Ported from Spectral Vowel Passage. Appended rather than grouped: 18 saved
//     projects use this plugin, and appending costs them nothing at all. ---
// Capture average: how many analysis frames the WASH spectrum is averaged over.
// GLOBAL here, unlike Passage -- for the same reason Overtone below is. It first
// landed per slot only because it was ported wholesale from Passage, where per
// slot is the house rule; the pairing with Capture point that justified it does
// not hold up. Capture POINT has to be per slot: it indexes into that slot's own
// grab, so a shared value would be meaningless. This is a smoothness dial, not
// an index, and per slot it cost eight identical edits on every project.
// The loss is real but small: a slot that only needed 1 now takes the same
// time-smear as the slot that needed 6. In a field whose slots blur into one
// another that costs far less than it would on Passage's separate stops -- which
// is exactly why Passage keeps it per slot.
// 1 is the original single-frame behaviour and the default.
slider28:1<1,6,1>Capture average (1 = one frame, higher = smoother)
// Overtone: bring one partial out far enough to be heard as a note of its own.
// GLOBAL here, unlike Passage -- this plugin is a field you sit inside, with one
// setting across the morph, rather than a route whose stops each have a character.
// Attenuates the others rather than boosting the chosen one: that is what a tract
// resonance physically does, and it means the control can only make the voice
// quieter. Harmonic 1 is exempt at any depth -- it is the drone.
slider29:0<0,64,1>Overtone harmonic (0 = off, 1 = fundamental)
slider30:24<0,48,0.5>Overtone lift (dB the chosen harmonic rises by)
slider31:1<0.5,4,0.1>Overtone width (harmonics either side)

// --- High cut. The mirror of Low cut, and the thing that makes an octave-up
//     Layer usable at all. Two deliberate differences from Low cut, both worth
//     knowing before you reach for it:
//       * it is applied in ABSOLUTE frequency, AFTER the pitch shift, where Low
//         cut is applied to the captured spectrum BEFORE it is resampled and so
//         slides with Pitch. A control whose job is "take the fizz off the top"
//         has to stay put while a layer transposes underneath it. Low cut keeps
//         its old placement so the saved projects sound exactly as they did.
//       * it shapes the VOICE as well as the wash -- partials fade out as they
//         cross it -- which makes the voice engine cheaper, not dearer.
//     20000 means off, and off is bit-identical to this control not existing.
slider32:20000<200,20000,10>High cut (Hz, 20000 = off)
// --- Layers: extra copies of the WHOLE morph -- voice and wash both -- at a
//     fixed interval from the main pitch, locked to the same morph position.
//     That lock is the entire point. Two instances on Shuffle land on different
//     slots and CLASH; a Layer is the same capture an octave away, so octaves
//     stack consonantly and Shuffle stays usable. Layers ride Pitch (and its
//     Drift/Ramp) as offsets from it, so the stack transposes as one.
//
//     FIFTEEN layers behind FIVE sliders, via the suite's nested-selector
//     pattern (the same shape as Drift target and Capture slot): every layer
//     sounds at once, and the selector only chooses which one the sliders below
//     are editing. Twelve of them are fixed intervals named on the selector --
//     four octaves each way, plus fifths and fourths -- so there is no
//     arithmetic and nothing to convert: you pick the interval and set a dB.
//     The first three are Custom, for anything the list does not name.
//
//     The named intervals live on the SELECTOR rather than behind a picker of
//     their own. They used to be a second nested list on every Custom layer,
//     which was mostly a duplicate of the octave entries already on the
//     selector -- two controls that looked like they did the same job because
//     very nearly they did. Putting them in the one list makes a fifth as
//     reachable as an octave and gave the freed slider to Solo.
//
slider33:0<0,15,1{Original (unshifted),Custom 1,Custom 2,Custom 3,4 octaves down,3 octaves down,2 octaves down,1 octave down,a fifth down,a fourth down,a fourth up,a fifth up,1 octave up,2 octaves up,3 octaves up,4 octaves up}>Layer
// Active/Inactive, per layer, and NOT the same thing as a level of -60.
// -60 is "this layer is silent because that is the level I want"; Inactive is
// "silence this layer and give it back to me later" -- the level you tuned is
// still there when you switch it on again. The suite convention (Vn Active in
// polyrhythm) and the reason it exists: a mute you can undo without having to
// remember a number. Both routes cost the same nothing at runtime, because the
// gate is the layer's gain and the whole layer's DSP is skipped when it is 0.
// Sits directly under the selector, ahead of the level: "is this layer in
// play" is the first question you ask about a layer, and it is a binary you
// can hear the answer to instantly. It also groups the two controls that
// apply to EVERY layer (active, level) ahead of the two that only apply to a
// Custom one (interval, pitch), so the always-present block is contiguous.
// This diverges from polyrhythm, where Vn Active comes last in the per-voice
// group -- deliberately: that group is flat and every voice is on screen at
// once, where this is a selector showing you exactly one layer at a time.
slider34:1<0,1,1{Inactive,Active}>Layer active
slider35:-60<-60,0,0.1>Layer level (dB, -60 = off)
slider36:0<0,1,1{Off,Solo}>Layer solo
slider37:-12<-96,96,0.1>Layer pitch (semitones, Custom layers)

@init
// ext_noinit = 1: @init runs ONLY on plugin load, so captured slots (and all
// memory) survive transport stop/play -- the whole point of a capture-based
// instrument. RUNTIME state (accumulators, positions, smoother) is reset
// explicitly on the transport play-edge in @block instead, so playback still
// restarts cleanly (and renders correctly) without wiping the captures.
ext_noinit = 1;
TWOPI = 6.283185307179586; PI = 3.141592653589793;
NSLOTS = 8; MAXFFT = 32768; INRING = 65536;
N_TARGETS = 24;  // Drift/Ramp targets. APPEND ONLY -- the index is saved in the project
                 // (slider17/23) AND is the bank position, so inserting one mid-list
                 // would silently repoint every saved selection above it:
                 // 0 Texture, 1 Spread, 2 Pitch, 3 Stereo width, 4 Low cut,
                 // 5 Voice level, 6 Overtone harmonic, 7 High cut, 8..18 layer levels
                 // (in layer order: the three Custom layers, then the eight octaves --
                 // the same order the Layer selector lists them in).
NLAYERS  = 15;   // pitch-shifted copies of the morph, all behind one selector
N_CUSTOM = 3;    // layers 0..2 are the free-interval Custom layers
                 // layers 3..14 are the fixed intervals, in selector order:
                 // -48 -36 -24 -12 semitones, -7 -5 +5 +7, then +12 +24 +36 +48
LAY_T0   = 8;    // target index of layer 0 -- targets LAY_T0+i map to layer i
// The ORIGINAL -- the unshifted morph every layer stacks around -- gets a bank
// slot of its own at index NLAYERS, so Active, Level and Solo apply to it
// exactly as they do to a layer. It is not synthesised by the layer loops (it
// is the base path), so the synthesis loops still run 0..NLAYERS-1 while
// anything reading the CONFIG banks runs 0..N_LAY_SLOTS-1.
LAY_ORIG    = 0;            // the original is FIRST -- it is what the rest stack around
LAY_FIRST   = 1;            // ...so the pitch-shifted layers run 1..N_LAY_SLOTS-1
LAY_CUSTOM0 = 1;            // Custom 1..3 at slots 1,2,3 (lay_semi is indexed slot-1)
N_LAY_SLOTS = NLAYERS + 1;  // config-bank entries: the original, plus the layers
FFTSIZE = 32768; NBINS = 16385;
// WA = analysis window length, FIXED and decoupled from Wash grain. The capture
// analysis (heavy: FFT + YIN) happens at WA; Wash grain only rebuilds the cheap
// synthesis grain. That makes Wash grain safe to AUTOMATE (no re-FFT, no buffer
// clear -> no dropouts), and Capture point scrubs this WA-length window in the grab.
WA = 8192;   // ~170 ms @ 48k: good spectral resolution, leaves ~0.5 s scrub range
// Capture average: frames step by half a window, and CAPAVG_MAX is what fits
// inside one grab while leaving the capture point somewhere to move.
CAPAVG_HOP = WA / 2; CAPAVG_MAX = 6;
// ACCLEN must exceed the LONGEST grain (W up to MAXFFT) plus a hop, or a long
// grain wraps the circular accumulator onto the read pointer (audible at the
// top of the Window-size range). 2*MAXFFT is comfortable headroom.
ACCLEN = 65536;
NHARM = 64; WHARM = 3072;          // 64 harmonics; ~64 ms YIN analysis window
WTBL = 8192; WTSCALE = WTBL / TWOPI;
// Voice stereo width: at full Stereo width the right-channel voice runs +-0.8%
// (~14 cents) detuned from the left, so L/R beat slowly -> width + de-robot
// shimmer. Tune by ear; bigger = wider/chorus-ier, smaller = tighter.
VOICE_DETUNE_MAX = 0.008;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Memory map: a RUNNING allocator, not hand-picked addresses. Every buffer is
// sized from the constants above, so NSLOTS (or any window size) can change
// without hand-editing offsets. The old fixed-address map collided the moment
// NSLOTS went past 4 (slotraw ran straight into slot_norm) -- this cannot.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
freemem = 0;
fftwork   = freemem; freemem += 2*FFTSIZE;    // FFT scratch (interleaved re/im)
inbuf     = freemem; freemem += INRING;        // live input ring
window    = freemem; freemem += WA;            // Hann of length WA (fixed analysis window)
synwin    = freemem; freemem += MAXFFT;        // sqrt-Hann synthesis grain (length W = Wash grain)
accL      = freemem; freemem += ACCLEN;        // overlap-add accumulator L
accR      = freemem; freemem += ACCLEN;        // overlap-add accumulator R
mtmp      = freemem; freemem += NBINS;
curmag    = freemem; freemem += NBINS;
phase     = freemem; freemem += NBINS;
doff      = freemem; freemem += NBINS;         // per-bin stereo decorrelation offsets
grainbuf  = freemem; freemem += MAXFFT;
// --- per-slot capture stores (ALL sized by NSLOTS -- this is what makes the
//     slot count a free dial) ---
slotmag   = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS*NBINS;  // wash (spectral) magnitudes
slotraw   = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS*MAXFFT; // raw captured audio (source of truth)
slot_norm = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;        // wash peak normalization
slot_harm = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS*NHARM;  // voice harmonic magnitudes
slot_f0   = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;        // detected fundamentals (Hz)
slot_hnorm= freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;        // voice peak normalization
slot_capavg   = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;    // Capture average (frames) per slot. The CONTROL is global -- every entry holds the same value -- but the storage stays per slot so compute_spectrum reads it the same way and the @serialize field keeps its byte format
ot_gain   = freemem; freemem += NHARM;         // per-harmonic Overtone gain (1 = untouched)
slot_cappoint = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;    // PER-SLOT capture point (0..1): where in each slot's grab to analyze. Each slot remembers its own scrub position.
// --- harmonic (voice) engine scratch ---
hph       = freemem; freemem += NHARM;         // resynth phase accumulators (voice A, left)
hphB      = freemem; freemem += NHARM;         // resynth phase accumulators (voice B, left)
hphR      = freemem; freemem += NHARM;         // voice A, right channel (detuned for width)
hphBR     = freemem; freemem += NHARM;         // voice B, right channel (detuned for width)
// Layers get their own phase accumulators -- one full set per layer, per morph
// bank (A/B), per channel. They cannot share the base banks: a layer runs at a
// different frequency, so a shared accumulator would be at the wrong phase for
// both. Sized off NLAYERS/NHARM like everything else here.
lay_hph   = freemem; freemem += N_LAY_SLOTS*NHARM;  // layer voice A, left
lay_hphB  = freemem; freemem += N_LAY_SLOTS*NHARM;  // layer voice B, left
lay_hphR  = freemem; freemem += N_LAY_SLOTS*NHARM;  // layer voice A, right
lay_hphBR = freemem; freemem += N_LAY_SLOTS*NHARM;  // layer voice B, right
// Layer config, derived in @slider (and per-sample when a level is drifting).
layer_ratio = freemem; freemem += 32;   // frequency multiplier from the interval
layer_gain  = freemem; freemem += 32;   // linear gain from the level (0 = off)
lay_semi    = freemem; freemem += 32;   // interval in semitones (the raw slider)
lay_db_base = freemem; freemem += 32;   // level in dB, before Drift/Ramp
lay_active  = freemem; freemem += 32;   // 1 = audible, 0 = muted but remembered
lay_solo    = freemem; freemem += 32;   // 1 = audition this layer alone
ser_scratch = freemem; freemem += 32;   // read-and-discard space for old @serialize layouts
lpr         = freemem; freemem += 32;   // pitch_ratio * layer_ratio, per grain
anbuf     = freemem; freemem += WHARM;         // mono analysis frame
yin_df    = freemem; freemem += WHARM;         // YIN difference function
yin_cmnd  = freemem; freemem += WHARM;         // YIN cumulative-mean-normalized difference
// --- sine wavetable (render-speed optimization): the voice engine's per-sample
// sin() calls become a table lookup. Internal only -- no sliders, no pitch or
// behavior change (frequency is set by the phase INCREMENT, which is untouched). ---
sintab    = freemem; freemem += WTBL+2;        // one sine period + a guard sample for interp
driftbag  = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;        // Auto-morph Shuffle bag (even random slot coverage)
// --- suite Drift: per-target config + runtime banks. Re-spaced 16 -> 32 when
//     the layer levels took the target count past 16, exactly as melody_phase
//     did at 28 targets: at 16 the banks are contiguous, so target 16 of one
//     bank IS target 0 of the next. Spacing is allocation only -- @serialize
//     writes n_ser_targets from each bank's own base, so the format is unmoved. ---
target_drift_up     = freemem; freemem += 32;  // CONFIG: up amount   (survives transport)
target_drift_down   = freemem; freemem += 32;  // CONFIG: down amount
target_drift_per    = freemem; freemem += 32;  // CONFIG: period (seconds)
target_drift_shape  = freemem; freemem += 32;  // CONFIG: 0 Sine / 1 Triangle / 2 Random
target_drift_phase  = freemem; freemem += 32;  // RUNTIME: 0..1 phase (reset on play)
target_drift_prev   = freemem; freemem += 32;  // RUNTIME: Random shape prev value
target_drift_curr   = freemem; freemem += 32;  // RUNTIME: Random shape target value
target_drift_offset = freemem; freemem += 32;  // RUNTIME: current offset (read at consumption)
td_active           = freemem; freemem += 32;  // @slider: 1 if a target has any drift configured
// --- Ramp: per-target config + runtime banks (32-slot-spaced, as above) ---
ramp_by_mem            = freemem; freemem += 32;  // CONFIG: signed `by` amount
ramp_dur_mem           = freemem; freemem += 32;  // CONFIG: duration (minutes)
ramp_delay_mem         = freemem; freemem += 32;  // CONFIG: start delay (minutes)
ramp_t_mem             = freemem; freemem += 32;  // RUNTIME: 0..1 ride progress (reset on play)
ramp_delay_elapsed_mem = freemem; freemem += 32;  // RUNTIME: start-delay sample counter
ramp_offset_mem        = freemem; freemem += 32;  // RUNTIME: ramp_t * by (read at consumption)
mod_active             = freemem; freemem += 32;  // @slider: 1 if a target has drift OR ramp configured
i = 0; loop(WTBL+1, sintab[i] = sin(TWOPI*i/WTBL); i += 1; );
// captured slots persist across transport play (one-time init)
slots_inited == 0 ? (
  slots_inited = 1;
  i = 0; loop(NSLOTS, slot_norm[i] = 1; slot_cappoint[i] = 0; slot_capavg[i] = 1; i += 1; );  // per-slot capture point defaults to 0 (earliest); @serialize restores/migrates
  i = 0; loop(NBINS, doff[i] = rand(1)*2 - 1; i += 1; );
  // have / n_used live in this ONE-TIME guard so captures survive transport
  // play (@init re-runs on every play; if these were reset there, a capture
  // made in one play would be orphaned on the next).
  have = 0; n_used = 0;
  // Drift CONFIG defaults live in the same one-time guard, for the same reason:
  // they must survive transport play. @serialize overwrites these with the
  // saved config on project load (the guard runs on fresh load, BEFORE the
  // @serialize restore — the exact order the captures above already rely on).
  i = 0; loop(N_TARGETS,
    target_drift_up[i]    = 0;
    target_drift_down[i]  = 0;
    target_drift_per[i]   = 30;
    target_drift_shape[i] = 0;
    ramp_by_mem[i]    = 0;   // Ramp CONFIG defaults (same one-time-guard rationale)
    ramp_dur_mem[i]   = 0;   // 0 duration = ramp inert until the user sets it
    ramp_delay_mem[i] = 0;
    i += 1;
  );
  last_target_select = 0;
  last_ramp_target   = 0;
  // Layer bank defaults. Every layer starts OFF, so the whole feature is inert
  // until a level is raised -- and one level IS the whole gesture, because the
  // interval is already decided by which layer you picked. These live in the
  // one-time guard for the same reason the captures do: they must survive the
  // transport, and @serialize overwrites them with the saved bank on load.
  lpi = 0; loop(N_LAY_SLOTS, lay_db_base[lpi] = -60; lay_active[lpi] = 1; layer_gain[lpi] = 0; layer_ratio[lpi] = 1; lpi += 1; );
  // The original defaults to UNITY, not off -- it is the sound, and a default
  // of -60 here would open every existing project silent.
  lay_db_base[LAY_ORIG] = 0; layer_gain[LAY_ORIG] = 1;
  // The three Custom layers start somewhere musical (an octave down, an octave
  // up, two octaves down) so that raising one is immediately worth hearing.
  lay_semi[0] = -12; lay_semi[1] = 12;  lay_semi[2] = -24;
  lpi = 0; loop(N_LAY_SLOTS, lay_solo[lpi] = 0; lpi += 1; );
  last_lay_sel = 0;
  last_cap_slot      = 0;   // per-slot Capture-point selector (mirrors the Drift/Ramp target selectors)
);
// Drift RUNTIME state (phase/prev/curr/offset) is reset here on load AND on the
// transport play-edge in @block, so drift restarts at phase 0 on every play
// (continues across stop) — matching the rest of the suite.
i = 0; loop(N_TARGETS,
  target_drift_phase[i] = 0; target_drift_prev[i] = 0;
  target_drift_curr[i]  = 0; target_drift_offset[i] = 0;
  ramp_t_mem[i] = 0; ramp_delay_elapsed_mem[i] = 0; ramp_offset_mem[i] = 0;
  i += 1;
);
eff_spread_hz = 0; eff_lowcut_hz = 0; eff_stereo_width = 0;   // seeded properly in @slider/@sample
// runtime state
cap_last = 0; first_slider = 1; inpos = 0; last_gsamp = -1; last_cappoint = 0;
cap_dirty = 0; capavg_dirty = 0; settle = 0; last_capavg = 1;   // debounce for Capture-point / Capture-average re-analysis (capavg_dirty = the re-derive covers ALL slots, not just the focused one)
// Captured spectra re-derive LAZILY -- only when actually stale (a fresh project
// load, or a capture-point scrub), never on an ordinary stop/play. In the normal
// capture-then-play flow nothing re-derives at all. Starts stale on load
// (@serialize restores the raw audio but not the analysis derived from it).
analysis_valid = 0;
rms_smooth = 0;   // slow auto-gain state (stable loudness, no per-grain pump)
cap_point01 = 0;  // analysis position in the grab (slider3); 0 = earliest
texture = 0.5; hlevel = 0.707; wlevel = 0.707;   // voice<->wash crossfade
i = 0; loop(NHARM, hph[i] = rand(1)*TWOPI; hphB[i] = rand(1)*TWOPI; hphR[i] = hph[i]; hphBR[i] = hphB[i]; i += 1; );
// Layer phases: random start per layer, R seeded from L (they part company only
// once Stereo width detunes them), mirroring the base banks above.
i = 0; loop(N_LAY_SLOTS*NHARM,
  lay_hph[i] = rand(1)*TWOPI; lay_hphB[i] = rand(1)*TWOPI;
  lay_hphR[i] = lay_hph[i];   lay_hphBR[i] = lay_hphB[i];
  i += 1;
);
// High cut / Layers runtime defaults: OFF, and off means the arithmetic below
// multiplies by exactly 1.0 -- bit-identical to the engine before they existed.
hicut_hz = 20000; eff_hicut_hz = 20000; hc_on = 0;
hc_top = srate*0.5; hc_lo = 1000000000; hc_inv = 0; hc_bound = srate*0.5;   // EEL2 has NO scientific notation -- 1e9 is a syntax error
hcbin_hi = NBINS + 1; hcbin_lo = NBINS + 1; hcbin_inv = 0;
nlay_on = 0; any_solo = 0; base_gain = 1;
// Overtone: flat gains (off), smoothers settled, ~30 ms to take the step out
// of a manual move -- each control is a gain change across dozens of partials.
i = 0; loop(NHARM, ot_gain[i] = 1; i += 1; );
ot_coeff = 1 - exp(-1 / (0.03 * srate));
ot_harm_base = 0; ot_depth_db = 24; ot_width = 1;
ot_h_smooth = 0; ot_db_smooth = 24; ot_w_smooth = 1;
ot_on_last = 0; ot_h_last = -1; ot_db_last = -1; ot_w_last = -1;
ot_boost = 1; ot_norm = 1; ot_bin = 0; ot_binw = 1; ot_f0bin = 0;
voice_detune = 0;   // right-channel voice detune amount (from Stereo width)
i = 0; loop(WA, window[i] = 0.5 - 0.5*cos(TWOPI*i/(WA-1)); i += 1; );   // analysis window (fixed)
morph_phase = 0; eff_morph01 = 0; last_automode = -1; last_ps = 0;
drift_was_playing = 0;   // Drift restart-on-play edge state
cur_slot = 0; nxt_slot = 0; xfade = 0;             // Drift: direct crossfade cur_slot -> nxt_slot
// Previous sample's morph pair, for the voice phase handover in @sample. -1 so
// the first sample records the pair without copying anything.
last_vsi = -1; last_vsj = -1;
bag_pos = 999; last_slot = 0; drift_primed = 0;    // Drift shuffle-bag state (primed lazily in @sample)
// Scramble the RNG per load: EEL rand() otherwise replays the SAME sequence
// every session, so Drift would wander the identical path each time you open the
// project. Advance it a sub-second-time-dependent number of steps so each load
// starts somewhere different. One-time, bounded, ~free.
_tp = time_precise(); i = 0; loop(floor(1 + (_tp - floor(_tp)) * 500), rand(1); i += 1; );

// wavetable sine: linear-interpolated lookup of one precomputed period. Only the
// sin() EVALUATION changes; phase accumulators/increments are untouched, so every
// harmonic stays exactly at (n+1)*f0 -- no detune, no inharmonic content. Phase
// accumulators wrap to [0, TWOPI), so fidx lands in [0, WTBL) and the guard sample
// at sintab[WTBL] covers i0 = WTBL-1.
function wtsin(ph) local(fidx, i0, fr) (
  fidx = ph * WTSCALE;
  i0 = floor(fidx); fr = fidx - i0;
  sintab[i0]*(1-fr) + sintab[i0+1]*fr;
);

// grab the most recent MAXFFT input samples into a slot's raw store
function grab_raw(slot) local(i, idx, base) (
  base = slot*MAXFFT;
  i = 0;
  loop(MAXFFT, idx = inpos - MAXFFT + i; idx < 0 ? idx += INRING; slotraw[base+i] = inbuf[idx]; i += 1; );
);

// FFT WA samples of a slot's raw, Hann-windowed, zero-padded to FFTSIZE. Uses the
// FIXED analysis window WA (not the synthesis grain W), so Wash grain never
// triggers this. Capture point picks WHERE in the ~0.68 s grab to look: 0 =
// earliest (the sustained vowel, since you react a beat after hearing it) .. 1 =
// the press-moment.
// Averages the MAGNITUDES of `Capture average` frames stepped through the grab
// by CAPAVG_HOP and centred on the capture point. At 1 frame this is the original
// analysis exactly, so nothing that already exists changes until the dial moves.
//
// Above 1 it fixes the wobble on re-spectralised material: a single frame freezes
// that instant's per-bin scatter and the wash replays it on every grain, so
// roughness that would average out over time instead recurs identically. Natural
// voice is unaffected -- successive frames look alike. Averaging lets the scatter
// cancel while the spectral shape, which every frame agrees on, survives.
//
// Magnitudes, not complex bins: summing complex would let frames cancel by phase,
// which is a comb filter rather than an average. The trade is time-smear, which
// is why it is a dial and not a default.
function compute_spectrum(slot) local(i, f, re, im, mx, rbase, mbase, kk, lo, hi, fstart, fb) (
  kk = max(1, min(CAPAVG_MAX, floor(slot_capavg[slot])));
  lo = slot*MAXFFT;
  rbase = lo + floor(slot_cappoint[slot] * (MAXFFT - WA));   // per-slot capture point
  hi = lo + MAXFFT - WA - (kk-1)*CAPAVG_HOP;                 // last start leaving room for kk frames
  fstart = rbase - floor((kk-1)/2)*CAPAVG_HOP;               // centre the span on the point
  fstart < lo ? fstart = lo;
  fstart > hi ? fstart = hi;
  mbase = slot*NBINS;
  i = 0; loop(NBINS, slotmag[mbase+i] = 0; i += 1; );
  f = 0;
  loop(kk,
    fb = fstart + f*CAPAVG_HOP;
    i = 0; loop(WA, fftwork[2*i] = slotraw[fb+i]*window[i]; fftwork[2*i+1] = 0; i += 1; );
    i = WA; loop(FFTSIZE - WA, fftwork[2*i] = 0; fftwork[2*i+1] = 0; i += 1; );
    fft(fftwork, FFTSIZE); fft_permute(fftwork, FFTSIZE);
    i = 0;
    loop(NBINS,
      re = fftwork[2*i]; im = fftwork[2*i+1];
      slotmag[mbase+i] += sqrt(re*re + im*im);
      i += 1;
    );
    f += 1;
  );
  mx = 0; i = 0;
  loop(NBINS,
    slotmag[mbase+i] = slotmag[mbase+i] / kk;
    slotmag[mbase+i] > mx ? mx = slotmag[mbase+i];
    i += 1;
  );
  slot_norm[slot] = mx > 0 ? mx : 1;
);

// YIN-lite pitch detect (+ subharmonic octave fix) then 64 harmonic magnitudes
// (direct DFT). VOICE end of the Texture knob -- harmonics at the exact detected
// f0 avoid the bin-drift that wrecks phase-coherent resynth from a frozen frame.
function analyze_harm(slot) local(base, ci, almin, almax, tmax, tau, ii, d, dd, run, thr, res, cand, lockon, best, pa, pb, pc, pden, psh, f0d, hn, hf, kk, re, im, ang, hannv, mag, mx, boff) (
  base = slot*MAXFFT + floor(slot_cappoint[slot] * (MAXFFT - WHARM));   // same per-slot point as the wash
  ci = 0; loop(WHARM, anbuf[ci] = slotraw[base+ci]; ci += 1; );
  almin = floor(srate/600); almax = ceil(srate/70); tmax = min(2*almax, WHARM-1);
  tau = 1;
  loop(tmax, d = 0; ii = 0; loop(WHARM - tmax, dd = anbuf[ii] - anbuf[ii+tau]; d += dd*dd; ii += 1; ); yin_df[tau] = d; tau += 1; );
  yin_cmnd[0] = 1; run = 0; tau = 1;
  loop(tmax, run += yin_df[tau]; yin_cmnd[tau] = yin_df[tau]*tau/(run+0.000001); tau += 1; );
  thr = 0.15; res = -1; cand = -1; lockon = 0; tau = almin;
  loop(almax - almin,
    res < 0 ? ( lockon == 0 ? ( yin_cmnd[tau] < thr ? ( lockon = 1; cand = tau; ); )
                            : ( yin_cmnd[tau] < yin_cmnd[cand] ? ( cand = tau; ) : ( res = cand; ); ); );
    tau += 1;
  );
  (res < 0 && lockon == 1) ? res = cand;
  res < 0 ? ( best = 1000000000; tau = almin; loop(almax - almin, yin_cmnd[tau] < best ? ( best = yin_cmnd[tau]; res = tau; ); tau += 1; ); );
  loop(3, (2*res <= tmax && yin_cmnd[2*res] < 0.25 && yin_cmnd[2*res] < 1.15*yin_cmnd[res]) ? res = 2*res; );
  (res > 1 && res < tmax) ? ( pa = yin_cmnd[res-1]; pb = yin_cmnd[res]; pc = yin_cmnd[res+1]; pden = pa-2*pb+pc; psh = (pden!=0)?0.5*(pa-pc)/pden:0; ) : ( psh = 0; );
  f0d = srate/(res+psh);
  (f0d > 20 && f0d < srate*0.5) ? (
    boff = slot*NHARM; mx = 0; hn = 1;
    loop(NHARM,
      hf = hn*f0d;
      hf < srate*0.5 ? (
        re = 0; im = 0; kk = 0;
        loop(WHARM, hannv = 0.5 - 0.5*cos(TWOPI*kk/(WHARM-1)); ang = TWOPI*hf*kk/srate; re += anbuf[kk]*hannv*cos(ang); im -= anbuf[kk]*hannv*sin(ang); kk += 1; );
        mag = sqrt(re*re + im*im);
      ) : ( mag = 0; );
      slot_harm[boff + (hn-1)] = mag; mag > mx ? mx = mag; hn += 1;
    );
    slot_f0[slot] = f0d; slot_hnorm[slot] = mx > 0 ? mx : 1;
  );
);

function capture(slot) (
  grab_raw(slot);
  slot_cappoint[slot] = cap_point01;   // stamp this slot with the current capture point; scrubbing later re-tunes only this slot
  slot_capavg[slot]   = cap_avg;       // and the frame count on screen
  compute_spectrum(slot);   // wash (spectral) analysis
  analyze_harm(slot);       // voice (harmonic) analysis -- same captured frame
  have = 1;
  (slot + 1) > n_used ? n_used = slot + 1;
);

function build_spectrum(useR) local(i, srcb, b0, fr, m, ph, woff, re, im, mir, lq, mb, hcw) (
  bsum_base = 0; bsum_all = 0;   // per-call totals, read by gen_grain after the L pass
  woff = eff_stereo_width * 0.01 * PI;   // eff_ = base + Drift offset (set per-sample)
  // Build a Hermitian-symmetric spectrum so ifft() returns a REAL signal:
  // fill bins 0..N/2 from the magnitude data, mirror them as conjugates.
  i = 0;
  loop(FFTSIZE/2 + 1,
    srcb = i / pitch_ratio;
    (srcb >= 0 && srcb < NBINS - 1) ? ( b0 = floor(srcb); fr = srcb - b0; m = curmag[b0]*(1-fr) + curmag[b0+1]*fr; ) : ( m = 0; );
    // Layers on the wash: the same magnitude spectrum read again at each layer's
    // own bin scale and summed in. This is why layers are nearly free here --
    // one extra interpolated read per bin, inside a grain that was already going
    // to be built, rather than a second pair of FFTs per layer. They share this
    // bin's random phase, which is exactly right for a phase-randomised bed:
    // where two layers land on one bin they are noise either way.
    m = m * base_gain;          // the original's own level: its gain, or 0 when something else is soloed
    nlay_on ? (
      mb = m;                     // keep the un-layered magnitude for the auto-gain below
      lq = LAY_FIRST;             // slot 0 is the original, already in m above
      loop(N_LAY_SLOTS-1,
        layer_gain[lq] > 0.0001 ? (
          srcb = i / lpr[lq];
          (srcb >= 0 && srcb < NBINS - 1) ? (
            b0 = floor(srcb); fr = srcb - b0;
            m += layer_gain[lq] * (curmag[b0]*(1-fr) + curmag[b0+1]*fr);
          );
        );
        lq += 1;
      );
    );
    // High cut, here in the OUTPUT bin domain -- i is an absolute frequency, so
    // the cut stays where it was set while Pitch and the layers move under it.
    hcw = 1;
    hc_on ? (
      i >= hcbin_hi ? ( hcw = 0; ) : i > hcbin_lo ? ( hcw = (hcbin_hi - i) * hcbin_inv; );
      m = m * hcw;
    );
    // Running totals with and without the layers. The wash is normalised per
    // grain to a fixed RMS, which would otherwise duck the base every time a
    // layer came in -- the same "the plugin keeps re-mixing you" problem the
    // voice had, but hidden inside an auto-gain nobody asked to be part of the
    // conversation. Parseval says grain RMS goes as sqrt(sum of squares), so
    // the ratio of these two IS the amount the layers inflated it, and dividing
    // it back out in gen_grain leaves the base sitting exactly where it sat.
    nlay_on ? ( mb = mb*hcw; bsum_base += mb*mb; bsum_all += m*m; );
    ph = phase[i] + (useR ? woff*doff[i] : 0);
    re = m * cos(ph); im = m * sin(ph);
    fftwork[2*i] = re; fftwork[2*i+1] = im;
    (i > 0 && i < FFTSIZE/2) ? ( mir = FFTSIZE - i; fftwork[2*mir] = re; fftwork[2*mir+1] = -im; );
    i += 1;
  );
  fftwork[1] = 0;                       // DC imaginary = 0
  fftwork[2*(FFTSIZE/2)+1] = 0;         // Nyquist imaginary = 0
  fft_ipermute(fftwork, FFTSIZE);
  ifft(fftwork, FFTSIZE);
);

function gen_grain() local(i, j, si, sj, mfr, ni, nj, bi, bj, sp, sumv, cnt, bj2, pk, thr, cutbin, sv, rms, gnorm, aw, lq2) (
  // slot selection (vsi/vsj/vmfr) is computed once per sample in @sample so the
  // wash and the harmonic voice always agree on which slot(s) they're playing.
  si = vsi; sj = vsj; mfr = vmfr;
  ni = slot_norm[si]; ni <= 0 ? ni = 1;
  nj = slot_norm[sj]; nj <= 0 ? nj = 1;
  bi = si*NBINS; bj = sj*NBINS;
  i = 0;
  loop(NBINS, mtmp[i] = (1-mfr)*(slotmag[bi+i]/ni) + mfr*(slotmag[bj+i]/nj); i += 1; );
  sp = floor(eff_spread_hz * FFTSIZE / srate);   // eff_ = base + Drift offset
  i = 0;
  loop(NBINS,
    sp <= 0 ? ( curmag[i] = mtmp[i]; ) : (
      sumv = 0; cnt = 0; j = -sp;
      loop(2*sp + 1, bj2 = i + j; (bj2 >= 0 && bj2 < NBINS) ? ( sumv += mtmp[bj2]; cnt += 1; ); j += 1; );
      curmag[i] = sumv / cnt;
    );
    i += 1;
  );
  ot_wash = (ot_on && ot_bin > 0) ? 1 : 0;
  pk = 0; i = 0; loop(NBINS, curmag[i] > pk ? pk = curmag[i]; i += 1; );
  thr = denoise_amt * 0.01 * 0.1 * pk;
  cutbin = floor(eff_lowcut_hz * FFTSIZE / srate);   // eff_ = base + Drift offset
  i = 0;
  loop(NBINS,
    i < cutbin ? ( curmag[i] = 0; ) : ( curmag[i] = max(0, curmag[i] - thr); );
    // Overtone on the wash. The harmonic peaks are still in the magnitude
    // spectrum, phase-randomised into narrow noise bands, so isolating one leaves
    // a pitched BAND rather than nothing -- breathier than the voice engine's
    // pure partial, still a definite note. Bins under the fundamental's own peak
    // are never lifted, mirroring harmonic 1 in the voice -- but they still take
    // ot_norm, so the whole wash recedes together rather than the band alone
    // rising out of an untouched bed.
    ot_wash ? (
      i > ot_f0bin ? (
        od = abs(i - ot_bin) / ot_binw;
        ow = od >= 1 ? 0 : (0.5 + 0.5*wtsin(od*PI + PI*0.5));
        curmag[i] = curmag[i] * (1 + (ot_boost - 1)*ow) * ot_norm;
      ) : (
        curmag[i] = curmag[i] * ot_norm;
      );
    );
    i += 1;
  );
  // Per-grain derivations for the two things build_spectrum needs beyond curmag:
  // where the High cut sits in output bins, and each layer's source-bin scale.
  // Both are read from the per-sample globals as they stand at grain time --
  // a grain is a snapshot, which is why Drift on either is smooth rather than
  // stepped: the steps are one grain apart and the grains overlap four deep.
  hcbin_hi  = hc_on ? hc_top * FFTSIZE / srate : NBINS + 1;
  hcbin_lo  = hc_on ? hc_lo  * FFTSIZE / srate : NBINS + 1;
  hcbin_inv = hc_on ? 1 / max(1, hcbin_hi - hcbin_lo) : 0;
  lq2 = 0; loop(N_LAY_SLOTS, lpr[lq2] = pitch_ratio * layer_ratio[lq2]; lq2 += 1; );
  i = 0; loop(NBINS, phase[i] = rand(1)*TWOPI; i += 1; );
  build_spectrum(0);
  rms = 0; i = 0; loop(W, sv = fftwork[2*i]; grainbuf[i] = sv; rms += sv*sv; i += 1; );
  rms = sqrt(rms / W);
  // Take the layers back out of the level the auto-gain is measuring, so the
  // base sits where it would with no layers at all and each layer arrives at
  // exactly the dB it was given. Without this the auto-gain reads the fuller
  // spectrum as "too loud" and quietly pulls everything down around it.
  (nlay_on && bsum_base > 0 && bsum_all > 0) ? ( rms = rms * sqrt(bsum_base / bsum_all); );
  // Slow auto-gain: smooth the grain RMS across grains so loudness is stable and
  // does NOT pump per grain. With the sqrt-Hann constant-power window below,
  // overlapping random-phase grains sum to steady power. Target ~0.07 grain RMS
  // -> ~0.1 output RMS (-20 dBFS). Freeze the smoother on silent grains (empty
  // slot) so switching back doesn't blip loud.
  rms > 0.0000001 ? ( rms_smooth <= 0 ? rms_smooth = rms : rms_smooth += 0.02 * (rms - rms_smooth); );
  gnorm = rms_smooth > 0.0000001 ? (0.07 / rms_smooth) : 0;
  build_spectrum(1);
  i = 0;
  loop(W,
    aw = accpos + i; aw >= ACCLEN ? aw -= ACCLEN;
    accL[aw] += grainbuf[i]   * gnorm * synwin[i];
    accR[aw] += fftwork[2*i]  * gnorm * synwin[i];
    i += 1;
  );
);

// Rebuild ONLY the synthesis grain window (sqrt-Hann, length W = Wash grain).
// CHEAP: no FFT, no YIN, no accumulator clear -- so Wash grain can be dragged
// or AUTOMATED without dropouts (old-length grains finish as new ones start).
function build_synwin(wlen) local(i) (
  wlen = max(64, min(MAXFFT, floor(wlen)));
  W = wlen; HOP = max(1, floor(W / 4));
  i = 0; loop(W, synwin[i] = sqrt(0.5 - 0.5*cos(TWOPI*i/(W-1))); i += 1; );
);
function clear_accum() local(i) (
  i = 0; loop(ACCLEN, accL[i] = 0; accR[i] = 0; i += 1; );
  accpos = 0; hopcount = 0;
);

// Fisher-Yates shuffle of the first n_used slot indices into driftbag. Drift
// draws its next slot from this bag, so it visits every captured slot exactly
// once per cycle (even coverage, random order) rather than a memoryless walk
// that clusters in the middle. Avoids an immediate repeat across the reshuffle
// seam (if the new first slot equals the one just visited, swap it back one).
function shuffle_bag() local(i, j, t) (
  i = 0; loop(n_used, driftbag[i] = i; i += 1; );
  i = n_used - 1;
  loop(n_used - 1,
    j = floor(rand(i + 1));
    t = driftbag[i]; driftbag[i] = driftbag[j]; driftbag[j] = t;
    i -= 1;
  );
  (n_used > 1 && driftbag[0] == last_slot) ? ( t = driftbag[0]; driftbag[0] = driftbag[1]; driftbag[1] = t; );
);

@slider
// Every slider read into a named global here; nothing downstream references a
// sliderN directly, so the layout can be reordered without desyncing the engine.
cap_slot      = max(0, min(NSLOTS-1, slider1));
cap_trig      = slider2; first_slider ? ( cap_last = cap_trig; first_slider = 0; );  // first @slider after load: sync cap_last so a saved-ON Capture-now never fires a silent capture on reload
// --- Per-slot Capture point (nested-selector, like Drift/Ramp targets). The
//     Capture-point slider is now PER SLOT: switching Capture slot saves the
//     old slot's point and loads the new slot's stored point into slider3, so
//     each slot keeps its own by-ear tuning. Editing slider3 writes the current
//     slot. Suppress the @block re-derive on a pure slot-switch (each slot is
//     already analyzed at its own point) by syncing last_cappoint. ---
last_cap_slot != cap_slot ? (
  slot_cappoint[last_cap_slot] = slider3 * 0.01;   // save the slot we're leaving
  slider3 = slot_cappoint[cap_slot] * 100;         // load the slot we're entering
  slider_automate(slider3);
  last_cap_slot = cap_slot;
  last_cappoint = slot_cappoint[cap_slot];          // no re-derive on switch: slot already analyzed at this point
) : (
  slot_cappoint[cap_slot] = slider3 * 0.01;         // capture a live scrub of the current slot
);
cap_point01   = slider3 * 0.01;
// Capture average is GLOBAL: one slider value stamped onto every slot, so
// switching slots neither saves nor loads it and there is nothing per-slot left
// to get out of step. The per-slot bank stays as the storage compute_spectrum
// reads and as the @serialize field (byte format unchanged) -- it just never
// holds eight different values any more. Stamping every @slider is also what
// flattens a project that was saved back when this WAS per slot.
cap_avg       = max(1, min(CAPAVG_MAX, slider28));
sca2 = 0; loop(NSLOTS, slot_capavg[sca2] = cap_avg; sca2 += 1; );
// Overtone is GLOBAL in this plugin -- see the slider block.
ot_harm_base  = max(0, min(NHARM, slider29));
ot_depth_db   = max(0, min(48, slider30));
ot_width      = max(0.5, min(4, slider31));
// --- Layer selector (nested-selector pattern, exactly like Drift/Ramp target
//     and Capture slot). Switching slider33 saves the visible sliders into the
//     OUTGOING layer's bank slot and loads the INCOMING layer's stored config
//     into them; otherwise it captures live edits to the selected layer. Every
//     layer sounds in parallel from @sample regardless of what is selected --
//     the selector is a view, never a mute. ---
lay_sel = max(0, min(N_LAY_SLOTS-1, slider33));
last_lay_sel != lay_sel ? (
  lay_db_base[last_lay_sel] = slider35;
  lay_active[last_lay_sel]  = slider34;
  lay_solo[last_lay_sel] = slider36;
  (last_lay_sel >= LAY_CUSTOM0 && last_lay_sel < LAY_CUSTOM0+N_CUSTOM) ? ( lay_semi[last_lay_sel-LAY_CUSTOM0] = slider37; );
  slider35 = lay_db_base[lay_sel]; slider_automate(slider35);
  slider34 = lay_active[lay_sel];  slider_automate(slider34);
  slider36 = lay_solo[lay_sel];    slider_automate(slider36);
  // The interval controls only mean anything on a Custom layer; an octave's
  // interval is fixed by which entry you picked. Load them anyway so the two
  // sliders always show the layer you are actually looking at.
  (lay_sel >= LAY_CUSTOM0 && lay_sel < LAY_CUSTOM0+N_CUSTOM) ? (
    slider37 = lay_semi[lay_sel-LAY_CUSTOM0]; slider_automate(slider37);
  );
  last_lay_sel = lay_sel;
) : (
  lay_db_base[lay_sel] = slider35;
  lay_active[lay_sel]  = slider34;
  lay_solo[lay_sel] = slider36;
  (lay_sel >= LAY_CUSTOM0 && lay_sel < LAY_CUSTOM0+N_CUSTOM) ? ( lay_semi[lay_sel-LAY_CUSTOM0] = slider37; );
);
// Show the interval controls only where they mean something: the picker on a
// Custom layer, and the raw semitones only when the picker can't say it.
// Solo applies to every layer; the raw semitones only to a Custom one, whose
// interval is not named on the selector.
slider_show(slider37, lay_sel >= LAY_CUSTOM0 && lay_sel < LAY_CUSTOM0+N_CUSTOM);
// Layer ratios and gains are DERIVED from the banks, so they are computed in
// @block, not here -- see the note there. Deriving them in @slider is what
// broke restore: @slider and @serialize have no guaranteed relative order.
dry_gain      = slider4 <= -60 ? 0 : pow(10, slider4/20);   // -60 = true silence
voice_db_base = slider5;                                    // base for Voice level drift
voice_gain    = slider5 <= -60 ? 0 : pow(10, slider5/20);
texture       = slider6 * 0.01;                              // 0 voice .. 1 wash
hlevel        = cos(texture * PI * 0.5);                     // equal-power crossfade
wlevel        = sin(texture * PI * 0.5);
grain_ms        = slider7;
spread_hz     = slider8;
pitch_semi    = slider9;                                    // base for Pitch drift
pitch_ratio   = pow(2.0, slider9/12);
stereo_width  = slider10;
voice_detune  = stereo_width * 0.01 * VOICE_DETUNE_MAX;   // voice width via detuned L/R unison
lowcut_hz     = slider11;
hicut_hz      = slider32;                                    // base for High cut drift (20000 = off)
denoise_amt   = slider12;
audition_mode = slider13;                                   // 0 focused slot, 1 morph
morph01       = slider14 * 0.01;
automorph_mode = slider15;
auto_time     = max(0.1, slider16);
automorph_mode != last_automode ? (
  last_automode = automorph_mode; morph_phase = 0;
  drift_primed = 0;   // force Drift to (re)build its shuffle bag on the next sample
);
slider_show(slider16, automorph_mode > 0.5);

// --- Drift target selector save/restore (nested-selector pattern, matching the
//     rest of the suite). Switching slider17 saves sliders 18-21 into the OLD
//     target's bank slot, then loads the NEW target's stored config into the
//     visible sliders. Otherwise it captures live edits to the current target.
//     All targets drift in parallel from @sample regardless of what's selected. ---
sel_target = slider17;
sel_up     = slider18;
sel_down   = slider19;
sel_per    = slider20;
sel_shape  = slider21;
last_target_select != sel_target ? (
  target_drift_up[last_target_select]    = sel_up;
  target_drift_down[last_target_select]  = sel_down;
  target_drift_per[last_target_select]   = sel_per;
  target_drift_shape[last_target_select] = sel_shape;
  slider18 = target_drift_up[sel_target];
  slider19 = target_drift_down[sel_target];
  slider20 = target_drift_per[sel_target];
  slider21 = target_drift_shape[sel_target];
  slider_automate(slider18);
  slider_automate(slider19);
  slider_automate(slider20);
  slider_automate(slider21);
  last_target_select = sel_target;
) : (
  target_drift_up[sel_target]    = sel_up;
  target_drift_down[sel_target]  = sel_down;
  target_drift_per[sel_target]   = sel_per;
  target_drift_shape[sel_target] = sel_shape;
);
// Per-target "is this target being driven at all?" flag — lets @sample skip the
// per-sample effective-value recompute (and keep the exact original CPU/output)
// for any target with no drift configured.
// --- Ramp target selector save/restore (nested-selector, mirrors Drift).
//     slider23 = target; 24 = per-target `by`; 25 = duration; 26 = start delay.
//     slider27 (Engage) is GLOBAL. All targets ride in parallel from @sample. ---
rsel_target = slider23;
rsel_by     = slider24;
rsel_dur    = slider25;
rsel_delay  = slider26;
last_ramp_target != rsel_target ? (
  ramp_by_mem[last_ramp_target]    = rsel_by;
  ramp_dur_mem[last_ramp_target]   = rsel_dur;
  ramp_delay_mem[last_ramp_target] = rsel_delay;
  slider24 = ramp_by_mem[rsel_target];
  slider25 = ramp_dur_mem[rsel_target];
  slider26 = ramp_delay_mem[rsel_target];
  slider_automate(slider24);
  slider_automate(slider25);
  slider_automate(slider26);
  last_ramp_target = rsel_target;
) : (
  ramp_by_mem[rsel_target]    = rsel_by;
  ramp_dur_mem[rsel_target]   = rsel_dur;
  ramp_delay_mem[rsel_target] = rsel_delay;
);
ramp_engaged = slider27;

// td_active / mod_active are derived from the drift and ramp banks, so they are
// computed in @block too, for exactly the same reason.
// eff_ defaults = base (so the first @sample and any inactive target read base).
eff_spread_hz = spread_hz; eff_lowcut_hz = lowcut_hz; eff_stereo_width = stereo_width;
drift_restart_free = slider22;   // 0 = restart on play (sync), 1 = free-running

@block
// Transport play-edge: immediate runtime setup -- rebuild window, clear the
// accumulator, reset runtime (captures themselves are NOT touched). With
// ext_noinit, @init no longer does this on play, so it's explicit here. The
// heavy spectrum/harmonic re-derive is NO LONGER done unconditionally here --
// it happens only when the analysis is actually stale, which is what stops the
// every-play crackle.
((play_state == 1 || play_state == 5) && (last_ps != 1 && last_ps != 5)) ? (
  build_synwin(floor(grain_ms * srate / 1000));
  clear_accum();
  inpos = 0; rms_smooth = 0; morph_phase = 0;
  last_gsamp = floor(grain_ms * srate / 1000); last_cappoint = cap_point01;
  last_capavg = cap_avg;
  // Snap the Overtone smoothers so a saved-on overtone does not chirp up the
  // series over 30 ms on every play.
  ot_h_smooth = ot_harm_base; ot_db_smooth = ot_depth_db; ot_w_smooth = ot_width;
  cap_dirty = 0;
  // (Drift reset is handled by its own transport block below — it must also
  //  catch loop-wraps / rewinds, which this capture-engine play-edge does not.)
  // Re-derive ONLY if the analysis is actually stale. In the normal
  // capture-then-play flow the spectra are already valid, so this does NOTHING
  // -- that is what kills the "crackle every time I hit play" (the old code
  // re-analyzed every slot on every play-edge, N heavy FFT+YIN passes crammed
  // into one block). When it IS stale -- a cold project load that hasn't been
  // scrubbed yet, including an offline render from cold -- derive in full here.
  // At the play-edge the accumulator was just cleared (no grains yet), so this
  // lump lands in the silent onset; and offline render has no real-time deadline
  // anyway, so it guarantees the render starts correct from sample zero.
  analysis_valid == 0 ? (
    si2 = 0; loop(n_used, compute_spectrum(si2); analyze_harm(si2); si2 += 1; );
    analysis_valid = 1;
  );
);
last_ps = play_state;

// --- Drift restart (Drift restart slider). "Restart on play" (default): drift
//     jumps to the start of its cycle the moment the transport begins playing
//     from a stop, so multiple tracks reset TOGETHER and their drifts stay in
//     sync (no clashy out-of-phase pitch motion). "Free-running": the transport
//     is ignored and drift evolves continuously — for a loop playing under/over
//     an unbroken drift, or any continuously-evolving texture. A loop repeat is
//     deliberately NOT a restart in either mode (drift flows straight across
//     loop boundaries); own edge state, independent of the capture play-edge. ---
drift_now_playing = (play_state == 1 || play_state == 5);
(drift_now_playing && drift_was_playing == 0) ? (
  // Ramp ALWAYS restarts its ride on transport play (its wind-down begins from
  // the current values each pass) — the suite's Speed-Ramp transport behaviour.
  ri = 0; loop(N_TARGETS, ramp_t_mem[ri] = 0; ramp_delay_elapsed_mem[ri] = 0; ri += 1; );
  // Drift restarts only in Restart-on-play mode (Free-running ignores transport).
  drift_restart_free == 0 ? (
    di = 0; loop(N_TARGETS,
      target_drift_phase[di]  = 0; target_drift_prev[di]  = 0;
      target_drift_curr[di]   = 0; target_drift_offset[di] = 0;
      di += 1;
    );
  );
);
drift_was_playing = drift_now_playing;

// Wash grain -> CHEAP: rebuild only the synthesis grain, immediately, every
// block if it's moving. No re-analysis, no clear -> safe to automate, no drops.
gsamp = floor(grain_ms * srate / 1000);
gsamp != last_gsamp ? ( last_gsamp = gsamp; build_synwin(gsamp); );

// Capture point -> HEAVY (re-FFT + YIN). Debounced so a by-ear scrub re-derives
// once, when it settles, in one lump. (Fresh load is handled at the play-edge
// above -- analysis_valid starts 0 in @init.) NOT an automation target.
cap_point01 != last_cappoint ? ( last_cappoint = cap_point01; cap_dirty = 1; settle = 0; );
// Capture average is global, so a change to it invalidates EVERY captured slot,
// not just the focused one. Flag that separately: the settle below needs to know
// which SCOPE to re-derive, and the two causes no longer share one.
cap_avg != last_capavg ? ( last_capavg = cap_avg; cap_dirty = 1; capavg_dirty = 1; settle = 0; );
cap_dirty ? (
  settle += 1;
  // On settle, re-derive all captured slots in ONE pass (debounced, so a by-ear
  // scrub recomputes once when it stops moving). Deliberately a single lump, not
  // sliced across blocks: one brief glitch during the scrub beats several smaller
  // glitches smeared into the audio you're listening to. Marks the analysis fresh
  // so the play-edge above won't redo it.
  settle >= 4 ? (
    analysis_valid == 0 ? (
      // Cold project load scrubbed before the first play: nothing is derived
      // yet, so derive ALL captured slots -- each at its OWN stored point.
      si2 = 0; loop(n_used, compute_spectrum(si2); analyze_harm(si2); si2 += 1; );
      analysis_valid = 1;
    ) : capavg_dirty ? (
      // Warm, and the GLOBAL Capture average moved: it describes how every slot
      // is read, so every captured slot has to come back through the analysis.
      si2 = 0; loop(n_used, compute_spectrum(si2); analyze_harm(si2); si2 += 1; );
    ) : (
      // Warm: only the focused slot's capture point moved (that one IS per slot),
      // so re-derive JUST that slot -- scrubbing one slot no longer retunes the
      // others (the bug this fix addresses).
      compute_spectrum(cap_slot); analyze_harm(cap_slot);
    );
    cap_dirty = 0; capavg_dirty = 0;
  );
);

(cap_trig > 0.5 && cap_last <= 0.5) ? ( capture(cap_slot); slider2 = 0; cap_trig = 0; sliderchange(slider2); );  // momentary: auto-release Capture-now so it can never be SAVED on (which re-captured silence on every load)
cap_last = cap_trig;

// --- Everything DERIVED from a @serialize bank is computed here, every block,
//     and deliberately not in @slider.
//
// This is the bug that ate an evening. @slider and @serialize restore state by
// two independent paths with NO guaranteed relative order, and on a track
// duplicate @init may not run at all. Deriving these in @slider meant that
// whenever @slider ran first, they were computed from banks that were still at
// their defaults -- every layer gain 0, every ratio 1, every drift target
// inactive -- and then @serialize restored the banks underneath them and
// nothing ever recomputed. The BANK was perfect and the arrays the audio engine
// actually reads were empty, which is a horrible failure to diagnose: the
// values are all still there, the plugin is simply not using them.
//
// Diagnostic signature, and it is a good one: the settings come back the moment
// you touch ANY control, because that runs @slider. Transport play/stop does
// not fix it (it never calls @slider), which is the tell that separates this
// from an @init / ext_noinit problem. "Wiggling the slider brought them back"
// is this bug and almost nothing else.
//
// @block has no such ordering problem: it runs every block forever, so by the
// first sample of audio these are correct no matter which order anything else
// happened in. The cost is 11 pow() and a 19-iteration loop per block, which
// against a 32768-point FFT per grain does not register.
//
// RULE FOR THIS FILE: a value derived from a serialized bank belongs in @block.
// Only values derived from a SLIDER may live in @slider, because those arrive
// with the slider itself.
layer_ratio[LAY_ORIG] = 1;   // the original is, by definition, unshifted
lz = 0;
loop(N_CUSTOM, layer_ratio[LAY_CUSTOM0+lz] = pow(2.0, lay_semi[lz]/12); lz += 1; );
// The twelve named intervals, in selector order, after the three Custom slots.
// Octaves are exact powers of two; fifths and fourths are the equal-tempered
// 7 and 5 semitones.
lz = LAY_CUSTOM0 + N_CUSTOM;
layer_ratio[lz+0]  = 0.0625;              // 4 octaves down
layer_ratio[lz+1]  = 0.125;               // 3 octaves down
layer_ratio[lz+2]  = 0.25;                // 2 octaves down
layer_ratio[lz+3]  = 0.5;                 // 1 octave down
layer_ratio[lz+4]  = pow(2.0, -7/12);     // a fifth down
layer_ratio[lz+5]  = pow(2.0, -5/12);     // a fourth down
layer_ratio[lz+6]  = pow(2.0,  5/12);     // a fourth up
layer_ratio[lz+7]  = pow(2.0,  7/12);     // a fifth up
layer_ratio[lz+8]  = 2;                   // 1 octave up
layer_ratio[lz+9]  = 4;                   // 2 octaves up
layer_ratio[lz+10] = 8;                   // 3 octaves up
layer_ratio[lz+11] = 16;                  // 4 octaves up
// Inactive is a gain of 0 and nothing else -- the dB the user set stays in the
// bank untouched, so switching back restores it exactly. Everything downstream
// gates on this gain, so an inactive layer costs no CPU.
// Solo. Any layer soloed silences every layer that isn't, AND the base voice --
// solo means "let me hear this one thing", so leaving the un-transposed morph
// underneath would defeat the point. Solo deliberately OVERRIDES Inactive: you
// solo something in order to hear it, and a solo that returns silence because
// the layer was also muted is a bad thirty seconds for anyone working by ear.
// A level of -60 is still silent, because that is a level, not a mute.
// The original is in the scan and in the gain loop, so soloing it works the
// same way soloing a layer does -- and so does muting it, or turning it down
// under an octave. It gets no interval because it IS the interval everything
// else is measured from; its gain lands in layer_gain[LAY_ORIG] and the base
// voice and base wash both read it as base_gain below.
any_solo = 0;
lz = 0; loop(N_LAY_SLOTS, lay_solo[lz] >= 0.5 ? any_solo = 1; lz += 1; );
lz = 0;
loop(N_LAY_SLOTS,
  lay_audible = any_solo ? (lay_solo[lz] >= 0.5) : (lay_active[lz] >= 0.5);
  layer_gain[lz] = (lay_audible < 0.5 || lay_db_base[lz] <= -60) ? 0 : pow(10, lay_db_base[lz]/20);
  lz += 1;
);
base_gain = layer_gain[LAY_ORIG];
// td_active gates the drift-advance loop; mod_active gates the effective-value
// recompute (drift OR ramp configured). A target with neither stays exactly at
// its base value -- bit-identical to no modulation. Same restore hazard: drift
// configured in a saved project simply would not run until you touched a
// control, which has been true since the drift work and was never noticed
// because touching a control is what you do next anyway.
dvi = 0; loop(N_TARGETS,
  td_active[dvi]  = (target_drift_up[dvi] > 0 || target_drift_down[dvi] > 0) ? 1 : 0;
  mod_active[dvi] = (td_active[dvi] || ramp_by_mem[dvi] != 0) ? 1 : 0;
  dvi += 1;
);

@sample
inbuf[inpos] = (spl0 + spl1) * 0.5;
inpos += 1; inpos >= INRING ? inpos = 0;

// --- Drift: advance each CONFIGURED target and compute its current offset.
//     Period is wall-clock SECONDS (this plugin has no musical clock). Shape
//     math (Sine / Triangle / Random value-noise) matches the rest of the
//     suite. Offset is asymmetric: positive half scales by `up`, negative by
//     `down`. Runs before every consumer below so grain/voice/output all agree. ---
di = 0;
loop(N_TARGETS,
  td_active[di] ? (
    dper = target_drift_per[di];
    dphase = target_drift_phase[di] + 1.0 / max(dper * srate, 1);
    dphase >= 1.0 ? (
      dphase -= 1.0;
      target_drift_shape[di] == 2 ? (
        target_drift_prev[di] = target_drift_curr[di];
        target_drift_curr[di] = rand(2) - 1;   // new random target in [-1,1]
      );
    );
    target_drift_phase[di] = dphase;
    target_drift_shape[di] == 0 ? (
      dshape = sin(TWOPI * dphase);
    ) : target_drift_shape[di] == 1 ? (
      dshape = dphase < 0.25 ? (4*dphase) : dphase < 0.75 ? (2 - 4*dphase) : (4*dphase - 4);
    ) : (
      dshape = target_drift_prev[di] + (target_drift_curr[di] - target_drift_prev[di]) * dphase;
    );
    target_drift_offset[di] = dshape > 0 ? dshape * target_drift_up[di] : dshape * target_drift_down[di];
  );
  di += 1;
);
// --- Ramp: while Engaged, each target with a duration advances its own ride
//     0..1 over that duration, after its own start delay; while Off, all rides
//     freeze and resume. The offset (ride * by) is computed every sample
//     (frozen when not engaged), and folds into the effective values alongside
//     the Drift offset below. ---
ramp_engaged ? (
  ri = 0;
  loop(N_TARGETS,
    ramp_dur_mem[ri] > 0 ? (
      rdelay_samps = ramp_delay_mem[ri] * 60 * srate;
      ramp_delay_elapsed_mem[ri] < rdelay_samps ? (
        ramp_delay_elapsed_mem[ri] += 1;
      ) : (
        ramp_t_mem[ri] += 1.0 / (srate * ramp_dur_mem[ri] * 60);
        ramp_t_mem[ri] > 1.0 ? ramp_t_mem[ri] = 1.0;
      );
    );
    ri += 1;
  );
);
ri = 0; loop(N_TARGETS, ramp_offset_mem[ri] = ramp_t_mem[ri] * ramp_by_mem[ri]; ri += 1; );

// Effective (base + Drift wander + Ramp ride) values, clamped to each param's
// real range. A derived global (hlevel/wlevel, pitch_ratio, voice_gain,
// voice_detune) is only overwritten when its target is being modulated at all,
// so a plugin with no Drift and no Ramp is bit-identical to before.
mo0 = target_drift_offset[0] + ramp_offset_mem[0];
mod_active[0] ? (   // Texture: internal 0..1, offset is in 0-100 Texture units
  eff_tex = max(0, min(1, texture + mo0 * 0.01));
  hlevel = cos(eff_tex * PI * 0.5); wlevel = sin(eff_tex * PI * 0.5);
);
eff_spread_hz    = max(0,  min(150, spread_hz    + (mod_active[1] ? target_drift_offset[1] + ramp_offset_mem[1] : 0)));
mod_active[2] ? (   // Pitch (semitones)
  // NOT clamped -- same reasoning as the twin in spectral_vowel_passage.
  // Pinning this to the slider's own +/-36 meant Drift and Ramp flattened
  // against the edge: the modulation kept moving and the sound stopped. The
  // spectral read is already bounds-guarded and the voice path only makes a
  // frequency, so an extreme value is inaudible rather than unsafe.
  eff_semi = pitch_semi + target_drift_offset[2] + ramp_offset_mem[2];
  pitch_ratio = pow(2.0, eff_semi/12);
);
eff_stereo_width = max(0,  min(100, stereo_width + (mod_active[3] ? target_drift_offset[3] + ramp_offset_mem[3] : 0)));
mod_active[3] ? ( voice_detune = eff_stereo_width * 0.01 * VOICE_DETUNE_MAX; );
eff_lowcut_hz    = max(0,  min(500, lowcut_hz    + (mod_active[4] ? target_drift_offset[4] + ramp_offset_mem[4] : 0)));
mod_active[5] ? (   // Voice level (dB)
  eff_vdb = max(-60, min(12, voice_db_base + target_drift_offset[5] + ramp_offset_mem[5]));
  voice_gain = eff_vdb <= -60 ? 0 : pow(10, eff_vdb/20);
);
// --- High cut (target 7). Derived into the four numbers both engines read:
//     hc_bound (stop entirely), hc_lo (start fading), hc_inv (fade slope) and
//     hc_top. OFF is not a special case downstream -- it is expressed as a fade
//     that starts at 1e9 Hz, so every partial multiplies by exactly 1.0 and the
//     Nyquist test compares against exactly srate*0.5, as it always did. The
//     fade (the top fifth of the cut) is there because this is a Drift/Ramp
//     target: a hard gate would step each partial off as the cut swept past it. ---
eff_hicut_hz = max(200, min(20000, hicut_hz + (mod_active[7] ? target_drift_offset[7] + ramp_offset_mem[7] : 0)));
hc_on = eff_hicut_hz < 19999.5 ? 1 : 0;
hc_on ? (
  hc_top = eff_hicut_hz; hc_lo = eff_hicut_hz * 0.8;
  hc_inv = 1 / max(1, hc_top - hc_lo);
  hc_bound = min(srate*0.5, hc_top);
) : (
  hc_top = srate*0.5; hc_lo = 1000000000; hc_inv = 0; hc_bound = srate*0.5;   // EEL2 has NO scientific notation -- 1e9 is a syntax error
);
// --- Layer levels (targets LAY_T0..LAY_T0+NLAYERS-1, one per layer in layer
//     order). Recomputed per sample only for a layer whose level is actually
//     being driven; otherwise @slider's value stands.
//     There is deliberately NO normalisation here. An earlier build held total
//     power steady as layers arrived, so the base gave a little back each time
//     one came in. That is defensible in the abstract and wrong in use: it
//     means the level you just set moves the level you set before it, so gain
//     staging becomes a chase against a soundscape the plugin is also editing.
//     The dB on the slider is the dB you get; levels add, and the meter is
//     where you find out. (The wash needed a matching fix -- see gen_grain.) ---
nlay_on = 0;
lz = 0;
loop(N_LAY_SLOTS,
  // Drift and Ramp move the level, never the switch: an Inactive layer stays
  // silent however far its level target wanders, so arming drift on a layer you
  // have muted cannot bring it back without you.
  (mod_active[LAY_T0+lz] && (any_solo ? lay_solo[lz] >= 0.5 : lay_active[lz] >= 0.5)) ? (
    eff_ldb = max(-60, min(0, lay_db_base[lz] + target_drift_offset[LAY_T0+lz] + ramp_offset_mem[LAY_T0+lz]));
    layer_gain[lz] = eff_ldb <= -60 ? 0 : pow(10, eff_ldb/20);
  );
  // nlay_on gates the pitch-shifted synthesis only, so the original never sets
  // it -- it is the base path, which always runs.
  (lz >= LAY_FIRST && layer_gain[lz] > 0.0001) ? ( nlay_on = 1; );
  lz += 1;
);
base_gain = layer_gain[LAY_ORIG];

automorph_mode > 0.5 ? (
  automorph_mode == 1 ? (
    // Sweep: triangle wander, morph runs 0 -> 1 -> 0 forever.
    morph_phase += 1 / (srate * auto_time);
    morph_phase >= 2 ? morph_phase -= 2;
    eff_morph01 = morph_phase <= 1 ? morph_phase : 2 - morph_phase;
  ) : automorph_mode == 2 ? (
    // Glide once: ramp 0 -> 1 and hold at the last slot.
    morph_phase += 1 / (srate * auto_time);
    morph_phase > 1 ? morph_phase = 1;
    eff_morph01 = morph_phase;
  ) : (
    // Drift = Sweep in shuffled order, crossfading DIRECTLY between consecutive
    // slots (never routing through the in-between ones). Advance the crossfade
    // cur_slot -> nxt_slot; when it completes, cur becomes nxt and we pull the
    // next slot from the shuffle bag (reshuffle once we've been through them all).
    // Each leg = auto_time/n_used, so one full pass = auto_time, each slot an
    // equal share. Direct A->B means every leg is the same single crossfade
    // regardless of which slots -> no whoosh, nothing in the middle to over-dwell.
    n_used < 2 ? ( cur_slot = 0; nxt_slot = 0; xfade = 0; ) : (
      // Lazily build the shuffle bag the first sample Drift actually runs with a
      // valid capture set -- NOT on mode entry, because on a reload that can fire
      // before the captures finish loading (n_used still 0), which stuck cur==nxt
      // on slot 0. Doing it here means n_used is always real.
      drift_primed != 1 ? (
        last_slot = -1; shuffle_bag(); bag_pos = 0;
        cur_slot = driftbag[bag_pos]; bag_pos += 1;
        nxt_slot = driftbag[bag_pos]; bag_pos += 1;
        xfade = 0; drift_primed = 1;
      );
      xfade += 1 / max(1, auto_time * srate / n_used);
      xfade >= 1 ? (
        cur_slot = nxt_slot; xfade = 0;
        bag_pos >= n_used ? ( last_slot = cur_slot; shuffle_bag(); bag_pos = 0; );   // through them all -> reshuffle
        nxt_slot = driftbag[bag_pos]; bag_pos += 1;
      );
    );
  );
) : ( eff_morph01 = morph01; );

// Shared slot selection -- BOTH engines read vsi/vsj/vmfr so they always agree.
audition_mode < 0.5 ? (
  // Focused-slot monitor: play exactly the capture slot, ignore morph.
  vsi = cap_slot; vsj = cap_slot; vmfr = 0;
) : automorph_mode == 3 ? (
  // Drift: DIRECT crossfade between the two consecutive shuffled slots (not a
  // position on the slot line), so it steps straight from one to the next.
  vsi = cur_slot; vsj = nxt_slot; vmfr = xfade;
) : (
  // Manual morph / Sweep / Glide: a position along the fixed slot line, blending
  // the two adjacent slots it sits between.
  vnu = n_used < 1 ? 1 : n_used;
  vmp = eff_morph01 * (vnu - 1);
  vsi = floor(vmp); vmfr = vmp - vsi;
  vsi >= vnu - 1 ? ( vsi = vnu - 1; vmfr = 0; );
  vsj = vsi < vnu - 1 ? vsi + 1 : vsi;
);

// --- Voice phase handover: hand the oscillator phases over with the slot. ---
// Bank A voices vsi, bank B voices vsj. When the pair steps on -- a Drift step
// between shuffled slots, or the morph position crossing a slot boundary -- the
// slot bank B had just crossfaded TO becomes bank A's, and A picked it up from
// its OWN phase accumulators: same magnitudes, same frequency, 64 partials all
// restarting mid-cycle, so the waveform STEPPED. That step is the click at slot
// changes. It reads as intermittent because its size is the difference between
// two unrelated phase sets -- sometimes inaudible, sometimes a pop -- which is
// what disguises a once-per-boundary bug as a random CPU dropout.
//
// Copying B's phases into A makes A resume the exact waveform B was mid-way
// through. B's own phases don't matter afterwards: it re-enters at zero level
// and fades up. A backwards step is the mirror case. The two are mutually
// exclusive because a two-slot bounce satisfies both tests at once, and running
// both copies would feed each bank the other's already-overwritten values.
// (Diagnosed and ear-tested in the sibling plugin, Passage, 2026-07-27.)
(vsi != last_vsi || vsj != last_vsj) ? (
  (vsi == last_vsj && vsi != last_vsi) ? (
    vcp = 0; loop(NHARM, hph[vcp]  = hphB[vcp]; hphR[vcp]  = hphBR[vcp]; vcp += 1; );
    // Every layer is a voice pair of its own, so every layer needs the same
    // handover -- otherwise the base voice steps through a slot change cleanly
    // and each raised layer clicks. One contiguous copy covers all of them.
    nlay_on ? ( vcq = 0; loop(N_LAY_SLOTS*NHARM,
      lay_hph[vcq]  = lay_hphB[vcq]; lay_hphR[vcq]  = lay_hphBR[vcq]; vcq += 1; ); );
  ) : (
    (vsj == last_vsi && vsj != last_vsj) ? (
      vcp = 0; loop(NHARM, hphB[vcp] = hph[vcp];  hphBR[vcp] = hphR[vcp];  vcp += 1; );
      nlay_on ? ( vcq = 0; loop(N_LAY_SLOTS*NHARM,
        lay_hphB[vcq] = lay_hph[vcq];  lay_hphBR[vcq] = lay_hphR[vcq];  vcq += 1; ); );
    );
  );
  last_vsi = vsi; last_vsj = vsj;
);

// --- Overtone: build the per-harmonic gain curve. ---
// Everything except a narrow window around the chosen harmonic is pushed DOWN, so
// one partial stands clear of its neighbours and reads as a pitch of its own.
// Attenuation rather than boost -- what a tract resonance physically does, and it
// means the control can only ever make the voice quieter.
//
// eff_ot_h is deliberately fractional: Drift and Ramp move it continuously, and a
// real singer's resonance sweeps rather than jumps, so at 7.5 the window sits
// across partials 7 and 8 and the emphasis hands over between them.
eff_ot_h = max(0, min(NHARM, ot_harm_base + target_drift_offset[6] + ramp_offset_mem[6]));
abs(eff_ot_h - ot_h_smooth)     < 0.0005 ? ot_h_smooth  = eff_ot_h    : ot_h_smooth  += (eff_ot_h    - ot_h_smooth)  * ot_coeff;
abs(ot_depth_db - ot_db_smooth) < 0.0005 ? ot_db_smooth = ot_depth_db : ot_db_smooth += (ot_depth_db - ot_db_smooth) * ot_coeff;
abs(ot_width - ot_w_smooth)     < 0.0005 ? ot_w_smooth  = ot_width    : ot_w_smooth  += (ot_width    - ot_w_smooth)  * ot_coeff;
ot_on = (ot_h_smooth >= 0.5 && ot_db_smooth > 0.01) ? 1 : 0;
(ot_on != ot_on_last || (ot_on && (ot_h_smooth != ot_h_last || ot_db_smooth != ot_db_last || ot_w_smooth != ot_w_last))) ? (
  ot_on ? (
    // --- A resonance is a PEAK: the chosen partial is LIFTED, the others are left
    //     alone. This first shipped inverted -- floored everything and returned the
    //     chosen one to 1.0, so the curve's maximum gain was 1 and nothing was ever
    //     lifted. Normalising to the PEAK is arithmetically the same as ducking the
    //     whole voice, and it is audible as one (Rozaya, 2026-07-28). Ear-safety is
    //     kept by normalising to POWER instead: lift the partial, then scale the
    //     bank so summed RMS is unchanged. It still can never get louder; it just
    //     stops sounding like the voice ducking. Full writeup in Passage, which
    //     carries the same engine. ---
    otg  = pow(10, ot_db_smooth/20);           // how hard the resonance lifts it
    otw  = max(0.5, ot_w_smooth);
    oi = 0; loop(NHARM, ot_gain[oi] = 1; oi += 1; );   // everything else stays put
    olo = max(1, ceil(ot_h_smooth - otw));     // harmonic NUMBERS (index = number - 1)
    ohi = min(NHARM, floor(ot_h_smooth + otw));
    oi = olo;
    loop(max(0, ohi - olo + 1),
      od = abs(oi - ot_h_smooth) / otw;
      ow = od >= 1 ? 0 : (0.5 + 0.5*wtsin(od*PI + PI*0.5));
      ot_gain[oi-1] = 1 + (otg - 1)*ow;
      oi += 1;
    );
    ot_gain[0] = 1;   // the fundamental is never lifted -- it is the drone
    opw = 0; oi = 0; loop(NHARM, opw += ot_gain[oi]*ot_gain[oi]; oi += 1; );
    ot_norm = opw > 0 ? sqrt(NHARM / opw) : 1;
    oi = 0; loop(NHARM, ot_gain[oi] = ot_gain[oi] * ot_norm; oi += 1; );
    ot_boost = otg;                            // the wash rebuilds the window itself
  ) : (
    oi = 0; loop(NHARM, ot_gain[oi] = 1; oi += 1; );
    ot_boost = 1; ot_norm = 1;
  );
  ot_on_last = ot_on; ot_h_last = ot_h_smooth; ot_db_last = ot_db_smooth; ot_w_last = ot_w_smooth;
);
// Wash-side position, in bins, off the morph-blended fundamental. Width is in
// harmonics for both engines, so here it becomes width x f0 in Hz.
(ot_on && have > 0) ? (
  ot_f0mix = ((1-vmfr)*slot_f0[vsi] + vmfr*slot_f0[vsj]) * pitch_ratio;
  ot_bpf   = FFTSIZE / srate;
  ot_bin   = ot_f0mix * ot_h_smooth * ot_bpf;
  ot_binw  = max(1, ot_f0mix * max(0.5, ot_w_smooth) * ot_bpf);
  ot_f0bin = ot_f0mix * 1.5 * ot_bpf;
) : ( ot_bin = 0; );

dryL = spl0 * dry_gain; dryR = spl1 * dry_gain;

// --- wash (spectral) engine: read overlap-add accumulator, refill per hop ---
wL = accL[accpos]; accL[accpos] = 0;
wR = accR[accpos]; accR[accpos] = 0;
accpos += 1; accpos >= ACCLEN ? accpos = 0;
hopcount += 1;
// Skip grain synthesis (gen_grain is HEAVY: two FFTSIZE iFFTs) when the wash is
// inaudible -- the mirror of the voice engine's "skip when Texture is full wash"
// guard below. At Texture 0 (pure voice) wlevel is 0, so wL*wlevel contributes
// nothing anyway; building grains for it was pure wasted CPU -- a real per-grain
// FFT load that could tip a busy project into occasional real-time dropouts
// (the "crackle on every slot, non-clipping" symptom). The accumulator still
// drains via the read-and-clear above, so raising Texture off 0 fades the wash
// back in cleanly over ~one grain. Output is bit-identical while muted.
hopcount >= HOP ? ( hopcount = 0; (have > 0 && wlevel > 0.0001) ? gen_grain(); );

// --- harmonic (voice) engine: TWO morph voices (vsi/vsj), each at its OWN
// captured pitch, crossfaded by morph. Now STEREO: when Stereo width (->
// voice_detune) > 0 the right channel runs a slightly-detuned copy, so L/R beat
// slowly -> real width + a shimmer that softens the robotic edge of pure
// additive harmonics. At width 0 it's the exact original mono path. Skipped
// entirely when the voice is inaudible (Texture at full wash, hlevel ~ 0) --
// that reclaims the whole per-sample harmonic loop when you live on the wash.
(hlevel > 0.0001 && have > 0) ? (
  fA = slot_f0[vsi] * pitch_ratio; normA = slot_hnorm[vsi]; normA <= 0 ? normA = 1;
  fB = slot_f0[vsj] * pitch_ratio; normB = slot_hnorm[vsj]; normB <= 0 ? normB = 1;
  bA = vsi*NHARM; bB = vsj*NHARM;
  voice_detune > 0 ? (
    // Stereo: left slightly flat, right slightly sharp (symmetric, so the
    // perceived pitch centre is unchanged). Separate R phase banks (hphR/hphBR)
    // run at the detuned frequency and drift against the L banks = width.
    fAL = fA*(1-voice_detune); fAR = fA*(1+voice_detune);
    fBL = fB*(1-voice_detune); fBR = fB*(1+voice_detune);
    vvAL = 0; vvAR = 0; vvBL = 0; vvBR = 0; vn = 0;
    loop(NHARM,
      hmA = slot_harm[bA+vn]/normA * ot_gain[vn];   // Overtone: 1 everywhere when off
      // High cut: hcg fades a partial out across the top fifth of the cut, and
      // hc_bound stops computing it entirely. With High cut off hc_lo is huge and
      // hc_bound is srate*0.5, so hcg is exactly 1 and the test is the original
      // one -- this path stays bit-identical for a project that never sets it.
      // Phase advances INSIDE the audibility test. The wrap below subtracts
      // TWOPI at most once per sample, which only keeps up while the step is
      // smaller than TWOPI -- i.e. while the partial sits under the sample rate.
      // Above that the accumulator climbed without limit, and wtsin indexes its
      // table straight off it, so a runaway phase read clean past the end of the
      // sine table into whatever memory follows. Nothing above Nyquist is heard
      // anyway, so not advancing what isn't read makes every step that happens
      // smaller than PI by construction (and does slightly less work). Needs
      // f0 x pitch above ~750 Hz to reach, but Pitch is a Drift/Ramp target.
      lypf = (vn+1)*fAL; hcg = lypf > hc_lo ? (hc_top - lypf)*hc_inv : 1;
      lypf < hc_bound ? ( vvAL += hmA*hcg*wtsin(hph[vn]);
        hph[vn]   += TWOPI*(vn+1)*fAL/srate; hph[vn]   >= TWOPI ? hph[vn]   -= TWOPI; );
      lypf = (vn+1)*fAR; hcg = lypf > hc_lo ? (hc_top - lypf)*hc_inv : 1;
      lypf < hc_bound ? ( vvAR += hmA*hcg*wtsin(hphR[vn]);
        hphR[vn]  += TWOPI*(vn+1)*fAR/srate; hphR[vn]  >= TWOPI ? hphR[vn]  -= TWOPI; );
      hmB = slot_harm[bB+vn]/normB * ot_gain[vn];
      lypf = (vn+1)*fBL; hcg = lypf > hc_lo ? (hc_top - lypf)*hc_inv : 1;
      lypf < hc_bound ? ( vvBL += hmB*hcg*wtsin(hphB[vn]);
        hphB[vn]  += TWOPI*(vn+1)*fBL/srate; hphB[vn]  >= TWOPI ? hphB[vn]  -= TWOPI; );
      lypf = (vn+1)*fBR; hcg = lypf > hc_lo ? (hc_top - lypf)*hc_inv : 1;
      lypf < hc_bound ? ( vvBR += hmB*hcg*wtsin(hphBR[vn]);
        hphBR[vn] += TWOPI*(vn+1)*fBR/srate; hphBR[vn] >= TWOPI ? hphBR[vn] -= TWOPI; );
      vn += 1;
    );
    hVL = ((1-vmfr)*vvAL + vmfr*vvBL) * 0.08;   // ~match the wash's ~0.1 RMS
    hVR = ((1-vmfr)*vvAR + vmfr*vvBR) * 0.08;
  ) : (
    // Mono (Stereo width 0): the exact original single-bank path, L = R.
    vvA = 0; vvB = 0; vn = 0;
    loop(NHARM,
      // Phase advance moved inside the audibility guard -- see the stereo path.
      lypf = (vn+1)*fA; hcg = lypf > hc_lo ? (hc_top - lypf)*hc_inv : 1;
      lypf < hc_bound ? ( vvA += (slot_harm[bA+vn]/normA)*ot_gain[vn]*hcg*wtsin(hph[vn]);
        hph[vn]  += TWOPI*(vn+1)*fA/srate; hph[vn]  >= TWOPI ? hph[vn]  -= TWOPI; );
      lypf = (vn+1)*fB; hcg = lypf > hc_lo ? (hc_top - lypf)*hc_inv : 1;
      lypf < hc_bound ? ( vvB += (slot_harm[bB+vn]/normB)*ot_gain[vn]*hcg*wtsin(hphB[vn]);
        hphB[vn] += TWOPI*(vn+1)*fB/srate; hphB[vn] >= TWOPI ? hphB[vn] -= TWOPI; );
      vn += 1;
    );
    hVL = ((1-vmfr)*vvA + vmfr*vvB) * 0.08;
    hVR = hVL;
  );
  // --- Layers: the same two morph voices again, at each layer's frequency
  // ratio, summed into the same output. BOTH voices, so a layer crossfades
  // between slots exactly as the base does and stays locked to the morph
  // position -- that lock is what makes an octave stack consonant where two
  // instances on Shuffle clash. Gated on the layer's own level, so a layer at
  // -60 dB costs nothing at all: skip the DSP, not merely its output (the
  // muted-layer rule behind the v2.18 crackle fix). Each layer keeps its own
  // phase banks; sharing the base's would put them at the wrong phase for both.
  // Upward layers get CHEAPER as they rise -- their partials cross Nyquist (or
  // the High cut) and stop being computed at all. ---
  nlay_on ? (
    lyk = LAY_FIRST;            // slot 0 is the original: the base path above
    loop(N_LAY_SLOTS-1,
      lyg = layer_gain[lyk];
      lyg > 0.0001 ? (
        lybo = lyk*NHARM;
        lyfa = fA*layer_ratio[lyk]; lyfb = fB*layer_ratio[lyk];
        voice_detune > 0 ? (
          lyfal = lyfa*(1-voice_detune); lyfar = lyfa*(1+voice_detune);
          lyfbl = lyfb*(1-voice_detune); lyfbr = lyfb*(1+voice_detune);
          lyv_al = 0; lyv_ar = 0; lyv_bl = 0; lyv_br = 0; vn = 0;
          loop(NHARM,
            hmA = slot_harm[bA+vn]/normA * ot_gain[vn];
            hmB = slot_harm[bB+vn]/normB * ot_gain[vn];
            lypf = (vn+1)*lyfal; hcg = lypf > hc_lo ? (hc_top - lypf)*hc_inv : 1;
            lypf < hc_bound ? ( lyv_al += hmA*hcg*wtsin(lay_hph[lybo+vn]);
              lay_hph[lybo+vn]   += TWOPI*(vn+1)*lyfal/srate; lay_hph[lybo+vn]   >= TWOPI ? lay_hph[lybo+vn]   -= TWOPI; );
            lypf = (vn+1)*lyfar; hcg = lypf > hc_lo ? (hc_top - lypf)*hc_inv : 1;
            lypf < hc_bound ? ( lyv_ar += hmA*hcg*wtsin(lay_hphR[lybo+vn]);
              lay_hphR[lybo+vn]  += TWOPI*(vn+1)*lyfar/srate; lay_hphR[lybo+vn]  >= TWOPI ? lay_hphR[lybo+vn]  -= TWOPI; );
            lypf = (vn+1)*lyfbl; hcg = lypf > hc_lo ? (hc_top - lypf)*hc_inv : 1;
            lypf < hc_bound ? ( lyv_bl += hmB*hcg*wtsin(lay_hphB[lybo+vn]);
              lay_hphB[lybo+vn]  += TWOPI*(vn+1)*lyfbl/srate; lay_hphB[lybo+vn]  >= TWOPI ? lay_hphB[lybo+vn]  -= TWOPI; );
            lypf = (vn+1)*lyfbr; hcg = lypf > hc_lo ? (hc_top - lypf)*hc_inv : 1;
            lypf < hc_bound ? ( lyv_br += hmB*hcg*wtsin(lay_hphBR[lybo+vn]);
              lay_hphBR[lybo+vn] += TWOPI*(vn+1)*lyfbr/srate; lay_hphBR[lybo+vn] >= TWOPI ? lay_hphBR[lybo+vn] -= TWOPI; );
            vn += 1;
          );
          hVL += lyg * ((1-vmfr)*lyv_al + vmfr*lyv_bl) * 0.08;
          hVR += lyg * ((1-vmfr)*lyv_ar + vmfr*lyv_br) * 0.08;
        ) : (
          // Mono (Stereo width 0): one bank, L = R, mirroring the base voice.
          lyv_al = 0; lyv_bl = 0; vn = 0;
          loop(NHARM,
            lypf = (vn+1)*lyfa; hcg = lypf > hc_lo ? (hc_top - lypf)*hc_inv : 1;
            lypf < hc_bound ? ( lyv_al += (slot_harm[bA+vn]/normA)*ot_gain[vn]*hcg*wtsin(lay_hph[lybo+vn]);
              lay_hph[lybo+vn]  += TWOPI*(vn+1)*lyfa/srate; lay_hph[lybo+vn]  >= TWOPI ? lay_hph[lybo+vn]  -= TWOPI; );
            lypf = (vn+1)*lyfb; hcg = lypf > hc_lo ? (hc_top - lypf)*hc_inv : 1;
            lypf < hc_bound ? ( lyv_bl += (slot_harm[bB+vn]/normB)*ot_gain[vn]*hcg*wtsin(lay_hphB[lybo+vn]);
              lay_hphB[lybo+vn] += TWOPI*(vn+1)*lyfb/srate; lay_hphB[lybo+vn] >= TWOPI ? lay_hphB[lybo+vn] -= TWOPI; );
            vn += 1;
          );
          lymix = lyg * ((1-vmfr)*lyv_al + vmfr*lyv_bl) * 0.08;
          hVL += lymix; hVR += lymix;
        );
      );
      lyk += 1;
    );
  );
) : ( hVL = 0; hVR = 0; );
// Base voice out of the way while a layer is soloed (exactly 1.0 otherwise).
hVL *= base_gain; hVR *= base_gain;

// --- crossfade voice <-> wash (equal-power), gate on having a capture ---
have > 0 ? (
  voutL = hlevel*hVL + wlevel*wL;
  voutR = hlevel*hVR + wlevel*wR;
) : ( voutL = 0; voutR = 0; );
voutL *= voice_gain; voutR *= voice_gain;

spl0 = dryL + voutL;
spl1 = dryR + voutR;

@serialize
// Persist captures across project save/reopen. Save only the RAW audio (source
// of truth) + counts; wash spectrum and harmonic analysis re-derive from slotraw
// on the first @block after load. Magic leads the stream so an old/absent blob
// degrades to "no captures" instead of scrambling state (CLAUDE.md gotcha).
// Magic:
//   7700001 -- 6 Drift/Ramp targets.
//   7700002 -- Overtone harmonic joined the target lists, making it 7.
//              The TARGET COUNT IS PART OF THE FORMAT -- the banks are written
//              back to back, so reading 7 where 6 were written walks the cursor
//              off the end of one and into the next. Hence a new magic rather
//              than an append. A 7700001 blob reads 6 and the seventh target
//              keeps its defaults.
//   7700004 -- current. High cut and the eleven layer levels joined the target
//              lists, making it 19. Same reasoning, same shape: a 7700002 blob
//              reads its 7 and the twelve new targets keep their defaults (no
//              drift, no ramp), which is exactly what those projects sounded
//              like. Targets were APPENDED, so every saved selector index still
//              points at the parameter it was pointing at. (7700003 was an
//              in-development layout with three layers and no octave stack; it
//              never left this branch, so nothing reads it.)
//   7700007 -- current. The original moved to the FRONT of the Layer selector
//              and of the Drift/Ramp target list, where it belongs by meaning.
//              Blobs migrate themselves (a rotate, below); the SLIDER LINE does
//              not and needs tools/morpher_migrate_layer_order.py.
//   7700006 -- The ORIGINAL gained a bank slot of its own (16 config
//              entries, 24 targets) so it can be muted, levelled and SOLOED
//              like any layer. A 7700005 blob is read at 15 and the original
//              seeded to unity -- which is exactly how it sounded, since the
//              base voice had no level of its own to have saved.
//   7700005 -- The named non-octave intervals moved onto the Layer
//              selector (15 layers, 23 targets), the interval picker became
//              Solo, and a solo flag per layer joined the stream. 7700004 is
//              still READ and migrated rather than dropped -- it existed for one
//              evening on one machine, but layers had been dialled in against
//              it, and resetting someone's work to save fifteen lines is the
//              same disrespect as moving their values silently.
ser_magic = 7700007;
file_var(0, ser_magic);
(ser_magic == 7700007 || ser_magic == 7700006 || ser_magic == 7700005 || ser_magic == 7700004 || ser_magic == 7700002 || ser_magic == 7700001) ? (
  n_ser_targets = ser_magic >= 7700006 ? N_TARGETS : ser_magic == 7700005 ? 23 :
                  ser_magic == 7700004 ? 19 : ser_magic == 7700002 ? 7 : 6;
  file_var(0, have);
  file_var(0, n_used);
  // Save/restore only the slots actually captured (n_used * MAXFFT), not the
  // full NSLOTS store -- so raising the slot count doesn't bloat the project
  // file with empty slots, and it scales with what's really in use. Backward
  // compatible: an old save that stored the full 4-slot block still restores
  // its captures (file_mem reads only n_used*MAXFFT, the leading slots, and
  // stops -- the trailing bytes were always empty tail slots anyway).
  file_mem(0, slotraw, n_used*MAXFFT);

  // --- Drift config, APPENDED at the end (safe without a magic bump). A
  //     pre-Drift project has the same 7700001 magic but no drift bytes; these
  //     reads run past EOF, and REAPER leaves the destination unchanged on a
  //     short read -- so old projects keep their captures and get default drift.
  //     Inserting mid-stream would misalign; appending is EOF-safe. ---
  file_mem(0, target_drift_up, n_ser_targets);
  file_mem(0, target_drift_down, n_ser_targets);
  file_mem(0, target_drift_per, n_ser_targets);
  file_mem(0, target_drift_shape, n_ser_targets);
  file_var(0, last_target_select);

  // --- Ramp config, appended after Drift. EOF-safe: a pre-Ramp project (drift
  //     only, or captures only) reads past EOF here and keeps default ramp. ---
  file_mem(0, ramp_by_mem, n_ser_targets);
  file_mem(0, ramp_dur_mem, n_ser_targets);
  file_mem(0, ramp_delay_mem, n_ser_targets);
  file_var(0, last_ramp_target);

  // --- Per-slot Capture point, appended after Ramp (EOF-safe append; no magic
  //     bump, same as Drift/Ramp were added). MIGRATION for projects saved
  //     before this field: they have no bytes here, so the read runs past EOF
  //     and REAPER leaves slot_cappoint unchanged. We detect that on READ with a
  //     sentinel dropped into slot 0 just before the read -- if it survives, the
  //     field was absent, so we seed every slot from the OLD global capture point
  //     (slider3). Pre-fix, all slots were analyzed at that one point, so this
  //     reproduces the old sound exactly. On WRITE (file_avail < 0) the sentinel
  //     is never set, so the real values are saved. ---
  file_avail(0) >= 0 ? ( slot_cappoint[0] = -999; );
  file_mem(0, slot_cappoint, NSLOTS);
  (file_avail(0) >= 0 && slot_cappoint[0] == -999) ? (
    seed_cp = slider3 * 0.01;
    scp = 0; loop(NSLOTS, slot_cappoint[scp] = seed_cp; scp += 1; );
  );

  // --- Per-slot Capture average, appended after Capture point. EOF-safe: a
  //     project saved before it existed reads past the end, keeps the sentinel,
  //     and seeds to 1 -- the single-frame analysis those projects were built
  //     on, so they sound exactly as they did. ---
  file_avail(0) >= 0 ? ( slot_capavg[0] = -999; );
  file_mem(0, slot_capavg, NSLOTS);
  // Absent field: seed every slot from the VISIBLE slider, exactly as the
  // Capture point migration above does. A project saved before this existed
  // has the slider at its default of 1, so it still reproduces the
  // single-frame analysis it was built on -- but it also means the setting
  // can be dialled in from outside without surgery on the saved blob.
  (file_avail(0) >= 0 && slot_capavg[0] == -999) ? (
    seed_ca = max(1, min(CAPAVG_MAX, slider28));
    sca = 0; loop(NSLOTS, slot_capavg[sca] = seed_ca; sca += 1; );
  );

  // --- Layer banks, appended after Capture average. EOF-safe like every append
  //     before it: a project saved before Layers existed reads past the end and
  //     keeps the @init defaults, which are "every layer off" -- exactly how
  //     those projects sound. Levels, then the Custom layers' semitones and
  //     picker positions (only N_CUSTOM of those are ever meaningful). ---
  ser_magic == 7700004 ? (
    // The one-evening layout: 11 layers (3 Custom + 8 octaves), an interval
    // picker per Custom layer, and no solo. Read it in its own shape, then
    // move the four upward octaves to where they live now -- the fifths and
    // fourths were inserted between them, so old 7..10 become new 11..14.
    // Walk DOWNWARD so a destination is never written before it is read.
    file_mem(0, lay_db_base, 11);
    file_mem(0, lay_semi,    N_CUSTOM);
    file_mem(0, ser_scratch, N_CUSTOM);   // the picker positions: read to keep the cursor aligned, then dropped
    file_var(0, last_lay_sel);
  ) : ser_magic == 7700005 ? (
    file_mem(0, lay_db_base, NLAYERS);
    file_mem(0, lay_semi,    N_CUSTOM);
    file_var(0, last_lay_sel);
  ) : (
    file_mem(0, lay_db_base, N_LAY_SLOTS);
    file_mem(0, lay_semi,    N_CUSTOM);
    file_var(0, last_lay_sel);
  );
  // Active flags APPENDED after the block above rather than slotted in beside
  // the levels they belong with. Order is the format here -- the reads are one
  // sequential cursor -- so putting them next to lay_db_base would misalign any
  // blob already written without them. Appending is EOF-safe: an older blob
  // stops here and every layer keeps its @init default of Active, which is the
  // state those projects were saved in.
  ser_magic == 7700004 ? (
    file_mem(0, lay_active, 11);
    // Migrate on READ only. On a write ser_magic is 7700005, so this is
    // unreachable there and cannot corrupt a save.
    file_avail(0) >= 0 ? (
      mig = 10;
      loop(4,
        lay_db_base[mig+4] = lay_db_base[mig];
        lay_active[mig+4]  = lay_active[mig];
        lay_db_base[mig] = -60; lay_active[mig] = 1;   // the vacated slots are the new fifths/fourths
        mig -= 1;
      );
      last_lay_sel >= 7 ? last_lay_sel += 4;
      mig = 0; loop(N_LAY_SLOTS, lay_solo[mig] = 0; mig += 1; );   // solo did not exist yet
      lay_db_base[N_LAY_SLOTS-1] = 0; lay_active[N_LAY_SLOTS-1] = 1;   // nor did the original's own slot (end-of-list; rotated below)
    );
  ) : ser_magic == 7700005 ? (
    // 15 layers and no slot for the original. Read what is there, then seed the
    // original to unity and audible -- which is what those projects sounded
    // like, since the base voice had no level of its own to save.
    file_mem(0, lay_active, NLAYERS);
    file_mem(0, lay_solo,   NLAYERS);
    file_avail(0) >= 0 ? (
      lay_db_base[N_LAY_SLOTS-1] = 0; lay_active[N_LAY_SLOTS-1] = 1; lay_solo[N_LAY_SLOTS-1] = 0;   // end-of-list; the rotate below moves it to the front
    );
  ) : (
    file_mem(0, lay_active,  N_LAY_SLOTS);
    file_mem(0, lay_solo,    N_LAY_SLOTS);
  );

  // --- Layer order migration, walked LAST so every older layout above has
  //     already been brought up to "the original lives at the end". Every blob
  //     before 7700007 has that order; this one rotates it to "the original
  //     lives at the FRONT", which is where it belongs by meaning -- it is the
  //     thing the other fifteen are measured against, and having to arrow past
  //     all of them to reach it was more to hold in your head than it was worth.
  //
  //     The permutation is a rotate right by one across the 16 config slots:
  //     old 15 (the original) becomes new 0, and old 0..14 become new 1..15.
  //     The Drift/Ramp target banks carry the same permutation over targets
  //     8..23, because target LAY_T0+i has always meant layer i.
  //
  //     THE SLIDER LINE CANNOT BE FIXED FROM HERE. slider33, slider17 and
  //     slider23 hold indices into these same lists and live in the project
  //     file, not the blob -- run tools/morpher_migrate_layer_order.py over the
  //     project folders to shift them. Without it the banks are correct but the
  //     selector and the Drift/Ramp targets point one entry low.
  (file_avail(0) >= 0 && ser_magic < 7700007) ? (
    rot = 0; loop(N_LAY_SLOTS,
      ser_scratch[rot] = lay_db_base[rot]; rot += 1; );
    rot = 0; loop(N_LAY_SLOTS,
      lay_db_base[rot == 0 ? 0 : rot] = ser_scratch[rot == 0 ? N_LAY_SLOTS-1 : rot-1]; rot += 1; );
    rot = 0; loop(N_LAY_SLOTS, ser_scratch[rot] = lay_active[rot]; rot += 1; );
    rot = 0; loop(N_LAY_SLOTS, lay_active[rot] = ser_scratch[rot == 0 ? N_LAY_SLOTS-1 : rot-1]; rot += 1; );
    rot = 0; loop(N_LAY_SLOTS, ser_scratch[rot] = lay_solo[rot]; rot += 1; );
    rot = 0; loop(N_LAY_SLOTS, lay_solo[rot] = ser_scratch[rot == 0 ? N_LAY_SLOTS-1 : rot-1]; rot += 1; );
    // the same rotate over targets LAY_T0..LAY_T0+N_LAY_SLOTS-1 of every
    // per-target bank; targets 0..7 are not layers and do not move.
    rotb = 0;
    loop(7,
      rotbase = rotb == 0 ? target_drift_up : rotb == 1 ? target_drift_down :
                rotb == 2 ? target_drift_per : rotb == 3 ? target_drift_shape :
                rotb == 4 ? ramp_by_mem : rotb == 5 ? ramp_dur_mem : ramp_delay_mem;
      rot = 0; loop(N_LAY_SLOTS, ser_scratch[rot] = rotbase[LAY_T0+rot]; rot += 1; );
      rot = 0; loop(N_LAY_SLOTS,
        rotbase[LAY_T0+rot] = ser_scratch[rot == 0 ? N_LAY_SLOTS-1 : rot-1]; rot += 1; );
      rotb += 1;
    );
    // and the three remembered selector positions
    last_lay_sel = last_lay_sel >= N_LAY_SLOTS-1 ? 0 : last_lay_sel + 1;
    last_target_select >= LAY_T0 ? (
      last_target_select = last_target_select >= N_TARGETS-1 ? LAY_T0 : last_target_select + 1; );
    last_ramp_target >= LAY_T0 ? (
      last_ramp_target = last_ramp_target >= N_TARGETS-1 ? LAY_T0 : last_ramp_target + 1; );
  );

  // TRACK-DUPLICATE FIX (CLAUDE.md nested-selector gotcha). On READ (load /
  // duplicate) file_avail(0) >= 0; on WRITE (save / undo) it's < 0. @serialize
  // is the one section guaranteed to run on both load AND duplicate, so after
  // restoring the banks we force the visible Drift AND Ramp sliders back to the
  // restored selected targets -- correcting any transient @slider clobber and
  // restoring the "visible slider == bank[selected]" invariant. sliderchange
  // (NOT slider_automate) here -- automate would write automation on load.
  file_avail(0) >= 0 ? (
    slider18 = target_drift_up[last_target_select];
    slider19 = target_drift_down[last_target_select];
    slider20 = target_drift_per[last_target_select];
    slider21 = target_drift_shape[last_target_select];
    slider24 = ramp_by_mem[last_ramp_target];
    slider25 = ramp_dur_mem[last_ramp_target];
    slider26 = ramp_delay_mem[last_ramp_target];
    // Per-slot Capture point: force slider3 to the restored slot's stored point,
    // and sync last_cap_slot so the first @slider doesn't mistake load for a
    // slot-switch (which would save the visible point into the wrong slot).
    cps = max(0, min(NSLOTS-1, slider1));
    last_cap_slot = cps;
    slider3 = slot_cappoint[cps] * 100;
    // Capture average is GLOBAL now, so this stamps the visible slider onto every
    // slot rather than loading one slot's value into the slider. A project saved
    // back when it was per slot flattens to the value the slider shows -- which
    // is the one its owner sees, and now the one they hear. @slider stamps too,
    // so it converges whichever of the two restore paths REAPER runs first.
    sca3 = max(1, min(CAPAVG_MAX, slider28));
    sca4 = 0; loop(NSLOTS, slot_capavg[sca4] = sca3; sca4 += 1; );
    // Layer interval pickers: ADOPT the restored positions, never fire on them.
    // The guard in @slider covers the ordinary load, but @serialize's read
    // branch is the one place guaranteed to run on both load AND track
    // duplicate, so syncing here closes the case where an early @slider ran
    // against pre-restore values. Firing on load would call slider_automate
    // during project open -- writing into an automation lane nobody touched.
    // Layer selector: same treatment as Drift/Ramp above. Force the visible
    // sliders back to the restored selected layer and sync last_lay_sel, so the
    // first @slider can't mistake a load for a selector switch and save the
    // wrong layer's values over the right one's. This is the section that runs
    // on a track DUPLICATE, where @init may not run at all -- without it the
    // selected layer is the one that silently zeroes.
    // Index off the BLOB's own last_lay_sel, NEVER off slider33 -- this is the
    // whole point of doing it here. Reading the slider assumes REAPER has
    // already restored the slider line, and the two restore paths have no
    // guaranteed order: if the slider is still at its default the visible
    // controls get loaded with the WRONG layer's values, and then the real
    // selector arrives, @slider reads it as a selector CHANGE, and the save
    // branch writes those wrong values into a different layer's bank slot.
    // The bank is scrambled and the next save persists the scramble.
    // Overwriting last_lay_sel from the slider made it worse by destroying the
    // one correct copy of the index we had just read back.
    // (The Capture-slot block below DOES read its slider -- correctly, because
    // last_cap_slot is not in the stream, so the slider is its only source.)
    slider34 = lay_active[last_lay_sel];
    slider35 = lay_db_base[last_lay_sel];
    last_lay_sel < N_CUSTOM ? (
      slider37 = lay_semi[last_lay_sel];
    );
    slider36 = lay_solo[last_lay_sel];
    sliderchange(-1);
  );
);
